F445|BYTE-SEARCHING A RUST RELEASE BINARY FOR A JSON KEY IS AN UNRELIABLE PROBE - IT RETURNED A FALSE NEGATIVE ON A FIELD THE BINARY DEMONSTRABLY EMITS|Verified on the running engine: plan_loaded now carries desc_sha, value a5467f9b, exactly as ab24ab31c intended. THE PROBE WAS WRONG. Both `strings -n 6` and `grep -ac` on the 235 MB release binary returned ZERO for desc_sha, and I ran a POSITIVE CONTROL that PASSED - retarget_discarded, sink_capped, complete_fix_completed, judge_verdict, tree_bytes and cap_base_secs were all found by the same command on the same file - while the source contained desc_sha five times. A positive control on sibling keys is exactly the discipline that is supposed to make a zero trustworthy, AND IT WAS NOT ENOUGH. The controls passed and the answer was still false. Most likely the literal is not stored contiguously for this key (rustc and the linker are free to merge, split or reconstruct string data, and serde_json's json! builds keys through machinery that need not leave a contiguous copy), so presence in the binary is not even the right question. THE CONSEQUENCE IS BIGGER THAN ONE FIELD: I used strings-style probes as a shipping check repeatedly today, and this proves that technique can report ABSENT for a change that shipped correctly. Had I trusted it I would have concluded ab24ab31c failed to build, and might have "fixed" a file that was already right. THE ONLY HONEST SHIPPING CHECK IS THE RUNNING BINARY'S OWN OUTPUT - the emitted event, not the bytes. That is the same lesson as L242 (a check is tested when its DETECTOR runs, never when its parser does) arriving from a different direction, and it is the FIFTH time today a zero from a wrong query nearly became a finding. ⇒ L251 A POSITIVE CONTROL PROVES THE QUERY CAN SEE SOME THINGS, NOT THAT IT CAN SEE THE THING YOU ASKED ABOUT - WHEN THE STAKES ARE "DID MY CHANGE SHIP", MEASURE THE BEHAVIOUR AND NEVER THE ARTEFACT
