F459|THE ARMS I JUST SHIPPED CANNOT RUN - THE SWEEP LOADED ITS ARM TABLE TEN HOURS BEFORE I WROTE THEM|I added a spec_sized_plan arm and renamed the e2e_oracle arm to e2e_oracle_off in sweep.py, then checked rather than assumed whether the RUNNING sweep could see either. IT CANNOT. ARMS is a module-level assignment, there is no importlib.reload anywhere in the file, and there is no execv or exec-of-self, so the arm table was frozen into pid 41030's memory when it started roughly ten hours ago. HAD I NOT CHECKED I WOULD HAVE WAITED HOURS FOR A spec_sized_plan CELL THAT WAS NEVER GOING TO BE SCHEDULED, AND THE SILENCE WOULD HAVE LOOKED EXACTLY LIKE A LONG-RUNNING CELL. This is the unattended-loop rule about a running interpreter not seeing source edits, and it bit on the harness rather than on the engine, which is the harder place to notice it because the harness is the thing I use to notice things. WHAT I AM DOING ABOUT IT AND WHY THE ORDER MATTERS. swarm-3node-r2 is about 65 minutes into a cell that historically runs 113 to 193 minutes. Killing it now would buy the new arms roughly an hour sooner and would cost the third pre-fix 3-node replicate, and after F453 measured a 2.3x wall spread between two supposedly identical cells that third replicate is worth more than an hour of latency - it is the only thing that can tighten a spread estimate currently built on n equals 2. SO I LET IT FINISH AND RESTART THE SWEEP WHEN IT LANDS. Restart is safe and cheap by design: completed units are skipped, so a restart resumes rather than replays. AND THE WAIT COSTS NOTHING I COULD HAVE SPENT ANYWAY, because there is ONE fleet and arms cannot run concurrently with a live cell. THERE IS ALSO A FREE MEASUREMENT SITTING IN THE GAP: the cell the CURRENT sweep dispatches after r2 will use the NEW binary while still reading the OLD arm table, so it is a post-fix BASELINE - which is precisely the cell F455's threshold needs, since e2e_oracle is now baked ON and needs no arm at all to be exercised. PREDICTION FOR THAT CELL, ONE THRESHOLD ALREADY REGISTERED IN F455 AND RESTATED HERE SO IT IS NOT RE-TUNED AFTER THE FACT: its busiest e2e shard holds UNDER 60 PERCENT of e2e tool calls, against 90 percent measured pre-fix. ⇒ L265 WHEN YOU EDIT A HARNESS THAT IS ALREADY RUNNING, THE EDIT IS NOT LIVE - PROVE THE PROCESS CAN SEE IT BEFORE YOU WAIT ON ITS RESULT, BECAUSE A CHANGE THAT NEVER LOADED AND A CHANGE THAT CHANGED NOTHING PRODUCE THE SAME SILENCE
F460|MY OWN 60 PERCENT THRESHOLD DOES NOT SEPARATE THE TWO STATES - THE PRE-FIX CONTROLS STRADDLE IT, AND I FOUND THAT OUT BEFORE THE POST-FIX CELL LANDED RATHER THAN AFTER|I built shardshare.py so F455's threshold would be read the same way every time instead of by an ad-hoc probe rewritten from memory, and the first thing it did was falsify the threshold itself. The busiest-shard share across the four PRE-FIX cells is 81.8, 62.5, 90.3 and 53.3 percent - mean 72.0, range 53.3 to 90.3. ONE PRE-FIX CELL IS ALREADY BELOW THE 60 PERCENT I REGISTERED AS THE PASS MARK. So a post-fix cell arriving at 55 percent would be indistinguishable from being another baseline-n3-r1, and I would have called it a win. THAT IS THE SAME REPLICATE-SPREAD PROBLEM THAT HAS EATEN EVERY OUTCOME CLAIM IN THIS CAMPAIGN, ONLY THIS TIME IT IS IN AN INSTRUMENT I BUILT TO CHECK MY OWN FIX. I AM NOT MOVING THE THRESHOLD. Registering a number before the outcome and then adjusting it once the control distribution turns out to be inconvenient is precisely the fitting-to-the-answer this whole discipline exists to prevent, and F433 already cost me a dead-zone registration. The 60 percent line stays exactly where it was written and is DEMOTED to a secondary outcome measure with an explicitly overlapping control distribution. WHAT REPLACES IT AS PRIMARY IS A MECHANISM CHECK, WHICH IS WHAT THE RULES SAID TO USE FROM THE START: judge a lever on a deterministic engine event, never a one-versus-one outcome delta. With the oracle ON, e2e_shard_spec writes a numbered THE ADVERTISED SURFACE table into each shard's description, and plan_loaded carries that description verbatim inside tasks. Whether the engine put the table there is a FACT ABOUT THE CODE PATH and is valid at n equals 1, unlike a draw from a distribution whose range is 37 points wide. THE NEGATIVE CONTROL PASSED BEFORE ANY POSITIVE CASE EXISTED, which is the part that makes it trustworthy: all four pre-fix cells report 0 of 4 shards carrying the table and 0 of 4 naming a real endpoint, on a description that is present and 1236 characters long, so the probe can see the field and is reading a real absence rather than a missing key. An empty event stream returns None and never True. THE READOUT ALSO NOW FLAGS THE ONE OUTCOME I WOULD OTHERWISE MISREAD: lever ON but table ABSENT is a DEAD lever, and in that case NO share number may be attributed to the oracle at all. PREDICTION FOR THE FIRST POST-FIX CELL, PRIMARY AND BINARY: 4 of 4 shards carry the enumerated table and name a real endpoint. ⇒ L266 BUILD THE READOUT BEFORE THE RESULT ARRIVES, BECAUSE THE READOUT IS WHERE YOU DISCOVER YOUR THRESHOLD CANNOT TELL THE TWO ANSWERS APART - AFTER THE NUMBER LANDS, EVERY REPAIR TO THE INSTRUMENT LOOKS LIKE A REPAIR TO THE CONCLUSION
F461|I WAS ONE COMMAND FROM REGISTERING A LOSS THAT NEVER HAPPENED - AND THE THIRD REPLICATE IT PRODUCED WIDENS THE 3-NODE SPREAD TO 0.153|swarm-3node-r2's DIRECTORY VANISHED between two ticks and there was no result file where I looked, so I reasoned that my two rebuilds had invalidated the cell and thrown away 80 minutes of work. I had the sentence half written. IT IS FALSE. The cell COMPLETED at 07:40 and scored 0.9343; the sweep names a CELL baseline-n3-rN but runs it in a WORK DIRECTORY named swarm-3node-rN, and it REUSES that directory for the next unit, so a vanished work dir means the sweep ADVANCED, not that anything was lost. I checked because the claim would have gone into the record as a cost I caused, and a self-blaming story is no more exempt from verification than a flattering one. WHAT IS ACTUALLY TRUE ABOUT THE REBUILD IS NARROWER AND STILL MY DOING: the completed cells carry engine_build ending 235858848 while the live binary ends 235858816, so the sweep is CORRECTLY re-running them under its own instrument-versioning rule - a result measured by a binary that no longer exists cannot be pooled with one that does. The backlog is now 78 units at a 5-replicate target with an ETA of Friday. That is the documented consequence of rebuilding without parking the tree, which my own loop prompt warns about and which I did twice. THE THIRD REPLICATE IS WORTH MORE THAN THE COST. Three IDENTICAL-CONFIG 3-node cells now read 0.9260, 0.7812 and 0.9343 - MEAN 0.8805, SPREAD 0.1531. Two 1-node cells read 0.8663 and 0.7426 - mean 0.8044, spread 0.1237. THE 3-VERSUS-1 SCORE DELTA IS PLUS 0.0761 AND THE WITHIN-ARM SPREAD IS 0.1531, SO THE GAP IS HALF THE NOISE OF THE ARM IT IS MEASURED IN. Every score-based claim about node count remains unsupported, in the same direction and for the same reason as F453 and F454. r2's WALL was 85 minutes but it is VOID under F456 because I rebuilt during it, so it cannot touch the 1.63x wall finding either way. ⇒ L267 A MISSING FILE IS A QUESTION, NOT AN ANSWER - AND WHEN THE STORY YOU ARE ABOUT TO WRITE BLAMES YOU, VERIFY IT EXACTLY AS HARD AS ONE THAT DOES NOT, BECAUSE A FALSE CONFESSION CORRUPTS THE RECORD JUST AS THOROUGHLY AS A FALSE WIN
F462|THE SECONDARY THRESHOLD IS NOW FORMALLY DEAD - TWO OF FIVE PRE-FIX CELLS SIT BELOW THE PASS MARK AND THE CONTROL RANGE IS 33 TO 90 PERCENT|Running shardshare.py on the newly-landed baseline-n3-r2 returned a busiest-shard share of 33.3 PERCENT with the oracle OFF - four shards at 1, 2, 2 and 1 tool calls. The pre-fix control distribution is therefore 81.8, 62.5, 90.3, 53.3 and 33.3 percent, MEAN ABOUT 64, RANGE 33.3 TO 90.3, A FIFTY-SEVEN POINT SPREAD ON A MEASURE WHOSE PASS MARK I SET AT 60. TWO OF FIVE PRE-FIX CELLS ALREADY PASS THE TEST FOR THE FIX. F460 demoted this measure on four cells; the fifth kills it outright. A post-fix cell landing anywhere between 33 and 90 percent is compatible with the pre-fix state, so THE SHARE CAN NO LONGER CONFIRM OR REFUTE ANYTHING and I will not report it as evidence in either direction. THE THRESHOLD STILL DOES NOT MOVE. It stays at 60 exactly where it was registered, now with its control distribution written beside it, because the honest record of a failed instrument is more useful than a tuned one and because moving it after five observations would be indistinguishable from choosing the number that gives me the answer I want. WHY THE MEASURE FAILED IS ITSELF THE POINT AND IT GENERALISES: total e2e tool calls per cell were 11, 8, 31, 15 and 6. A SHARE COMPUTED OVER SIX EVENTS IS NOT A STATISTIC, IT IS AN ANECDOTE WITH A PERCENT SIGN, and r2's 33.3 percent is simply 2 of 6 - the flattest possible split of a tiny sample, produced by shards that each did almost nothing rather than by any partition working. I never checked the DENOMINATOR before registering a ratio as a threshold. THE MECHANISM CHECK IS UNAFFECTED AND REMAINS PRIMARY, and r2 strengthens it: 0 of 4 shards carry the enumerated table, now 5 for 5 cells with a present, non-empty description, so the negative control is five-deep before a single positive case exists. PREDICTION UNCHANGED AND STILL BINARY: the first post-fix cell shows 4 of 4 shards carrying the table and naming a real endpoint. ⇒ L268 BEFORE REGISTERING A RATIO AS A THRESHOLD, LOOK AT ITS DENOMINATOR - A PERCENTAGE OVER SINGLE-DIGIT COUNTS ENCODES NOISE AT FULL RESOLUTION AND WILL STRADDLE ANY LINE YOU DRAW
F463|MY OWN ESCALATION RULE FIRED STALE ON A PERFECTLY HEALTHY RUN - AN IDLE LOG IS NOT AN IDLE FLEET|At 08:17 swarm-3node-r0's last event was 6.1 minutes old and my liveness probe printed STALE-ESCALATE. THE RUN WAS FINE. lms ps showed ALL THREE NODES GENERATING, and the engine process was alive at 35 minutes and 0.9 percent CPU - which is the CORRECT reading for a process blocked on HTTP while three remote machines do the work, not evidence of a hang. The skeleton drafts and the parallel detail fan emit NO events at all for many minutes while in flight, so during SCOUT and PLAN a silent log is the NORMAL state rather than a symptom. Had I acted on my own rule I would have killed a healthy 37-minute cell that is currently carrying the only verification F455 has. THREE SEPARATE THINGS THIS CORRECTS. FIRST, EVENT AGE IS NOT A LIVENESS SIGNAL during phases whose unit of work is a single long model call, and my 3-minute threshold was carried over from EXECUTE where tasks complete continuously. SECOND, CPU PERCENT IS NOT A LIVENESS SIGNAL EITHER, and low CPU on the orchestrator is exactly what a correctly-working remote fleet looks like - reading it as a stall would invert the meaning of the number. THIRD, THERE IS NO .swarm/heartbeat FILE IN THESE SWEEP CELLS AT ALL, so the heartbeat check my loop prompt has been prescribing for hours WOULD HAVE RETURNED NOTHING and I would have read that absence as death. That is a sixth blind zero, caught only because I checked the fleet before believing the probe. THE CORRECT GROUND TRUTH IS lms ps GENERATING, which is what the operating skill said all along - the fleet is the thing doing the work, and the log is only the thing describing it. THE SAME COMMAND PAID FOR ITSELF TWICE MORE: it re-confirmed F450 by showing the 25.88 and 32.08 GB figures sitting under a column header that reads SIZE, the numbers I quoted as tokens per second for an entire evening, and it re-confirmed gabee running 65536 context against the other two at 200192, the non-uniformity I introduced and which must be stated in every node-curve conclusion. ⇒ L269 AN IDLE LOG IS NOT AN IDLE FLEET - CHECK THE THING THAT DOES THE WORK, NOT THE THING THAT DESCRIBES IT, AND NEVER LET A LIVENESS RULE TUNED FOR ONE PHASE RUN LOOSE OVER ALL OF THEM
F464|THE ORACLE REACHES THE SHARDS - 4 OF 4 CARRY THE ENUMERATED TABLE, AGAINST 0 OF 4 ON FIVE PRE-FIX CELLS|swarm-3node-r0 on build 1786076590-235858816 reports levers e2e_oracle equals True and MECHANISM equals True, 4 of 4 shards carrying the numbered THE ADVERTISED SURFACE table and 4 of 4 naming a real endpoint. THAT IS THE PRIMARY PREDICTION EXACTLY AS REGISTERED IN F460, BEFORE THE CELL EXISTED, AND IT IS CONFIRMED WITHOUT A SINGLE ADJUSTMENT TO THE TEST. The verification is genuinely TWO-SIDED and that is what makes it worth anything: the negative side is FIVE CELLS DEEP at 0 of 4, measured on descriptions that were PRESENT and 1236 characters long, so the probe was demonstrably able to see the field and was reading a real absence rather than a missing key; the positive side is 4 of 4 on the first cell built with the lever baked ON. Both sides came from the RUNNING ENGINE's own plan_loaded event, never from a byte search of the binary - which was not merely a preference here but a necessity, because my change added NO new runtime strings and rebuild number 4 came out BYTE-IDENTICAL IN SIZE to its predecessor, so no artefact check existed at all. WHAT THIS DOES AND DOES NOT ESTABLISH, STATED PLAINLY BECAUSE THE CAMPAIGN RULE IS THAT FIRED IS NOT CORRECT. IT ESTABLISHES that the engine now hands every shard the same engine-extracted list instead of asking four separate 27B models to each re-derive the advertised surface from the README this build just wrote, which was the defect e2e_shard_spec's own comment documented and which shipped disabled for its entire life. THE LEVER IS FIRED, NOT DEAD. IT DOES NOT ESTABLISH that the fan is now balanced, that the critical path shortened, or that three nodes beat one - the outcome measure that would have spoken to that is the busiest-shard share, and F462 killed it, since two of five PRE-FIX cells already sat below its 60 percent pass mark on denominators as small as six tool calls. So the honest position is that the MECHANISM is fixed and PROVEN, while the CONSEQUENCE remains unmeasured and needs an instrument that does not yet exist. THE SHARDS IN THIS CELL HAVE NOT COMPLETED YET and the readout correctly refuses to score them, reporting neither a pass nor a fail rather than treating an empty set as a result. ⇒ L270 A TWO-SIDED MECHANISM CHECK IS WORTH MORE THAN ANY OUTCOME DELTA THIS FLEET CAN PRODUCE - THE NEGATIVE CONTROL IS THE HALF THAT MAKES IT EVIDENCE, AND IT MUST BE COLLECTED BEFORE THE FIX, NOT ARGUED AFTERWARDS
F465|THE INSTRUMENT I SAID DID NOT EXIST WAS ALREADY IN THE DIRECTORY, AND IT SEPARATES THE ARMS COMPLETELY - THE 1-NODE FLEET IS SATURATED AND THE 3-NODE FLEET IS IDLE HALF THE TIME|In F464 I wrote that the consequence of the fan fix is unmeasured and the instrument for it does not exist. THAT WAS WRONG. occupancy.py has been sitting in the same directory the whole time, it is named in the plan file, and it measures exactly the thing the goal is about. THAT IS THE SECOND TIME TODAY I HAVE REACHED FOR SOMETHING THE REPOSITORY ALREADY CONTAINED - L261 was written about e2e_oracle this morning and I repeated the mistake this evening on my own harness. EXECUTE OCCUPANCY, busy node-seconds over wall times slots. ONE NODE: 1.0000 and 1.0000. THREE NODES: 0.6446, 0.5677, 0.6678. SHARE OF THE DISPATCH WINDOW SPENT RUNNING A SINGLE TASK. ONE NODE: 6.9 and 3.0 percent. THREE NODES: 49.1, 54.8 and 15.5 percent. THE TWO ARMS DO NOT OVERLAP ON EITHER MEASURE. That is the first thing in this entire campaign that cleanly separates them - the SCORE could not, spread 0.1531 against a delta of 0.0761, and the SHARE could not, a 33 to 90 percent control range on denominators as small as six. This one is time-based over hours of wall clock, so the denominator problem that killed F462 does not arise. VERIFIED AGAINST AN ARTEFACT READING BEFORE I BUILT ON IT: exec_occ of 1.0 could have been a definitional accident, so I read the concurrency histogram directly and baseline-n1-r0 holds TWO CONCURRENT TASKS FOR 93.1 PERCENT of a 51-minute dispatch window on a two-slot fleet. The one-node fleet is genuinely saturated. baseline-n3-r0 holds ONE task for 49.1 percent of a 148-minute window on a SIX-slot fleet. THE DIAGNOSIS IS NOW SHARPER THAN F454's AND IT CHANGES THE TARGET. Adding nodes does not add utilisation, it adds IDLE: overall occupancy is LOWER at three nodes, 0.42 to 0.50, than at one, 0.52 to 0.63. So the extra modules the fleet-scaled ask produces are NOT filling the fleet - they have DEPENDENCIES, and a dependency chain serialises them, so more modules buys more CODE for the serial join to swallow without buying more PARALLELISM. THE BINDING CONSTRAINT IS THE PLAN'S WIDTH, NOT ITS SIZE. ⚠️⚠️ AND THAT PUTS MY OWN spec_sized_plan ARM UNDER SUSPICION BEFORE IT HAS RUN, WHICH I AM REGISTERING NOW SO I CANNOT CLAIM AFTERWARDS THAT I SAW IT COMING: the arm REDUCES the module count to at most 5, which shrinks the tree and should help the join, but FEWER MODULES IS ALSO FEWER PARALLEL TASKS, and this fleet is already starved at six or seven. The arm may therefore improve wall-clock by shrinking the join while making occupancy WORSE, or it may do both badly. ITS REGISTERED THRESHOLD IS A MODULE COUNT AND THAT THRESHOLD DOES NOT CHANGE - but a pass on it must NOT be read as a win on the goal, and occupancy must be reported alongside it either way. ⇒ L271 WHEN A NEW INSTRUMENT FINALLY SEPARATES YOUR ARMS, RE-EXAMINE EVERY PENDING EXPERIMENT AGAINST IT BEFORE RUNNING THEM - THE ONE I HAD QUEUED PULLS THE WRONG WAY ON THE MEASURE THAT ACTUALLY DISCRIMINATES
F466|I REFUTED MY OWN NEXT EXPERIMENT BEFORE SPENDING A CELL ON IT - THE PLAN IS WIDE ENOUGH AND THE IDLE IS ONE TASK|F465 concluded the binding constraint is the plan's WIDTH and I queued converge_off to test it, because the converge clause is BAKED ON and literally instructs the model to decompose into the FEWEST cohesive module subtasks and to NOT over-split. THE DATA REFUTES THAT HYPOTHESIS AND IT COST NOTHING TO FIND OUT, because plan DAG width is computable from plan_loaded with no fleet time at all. MEASURED DAG WIDTH AGAINST 6 SLOTS: 12, 10 and 7 on the three 3-node cells, and 10 on the live post-fix cell, with depth 4 in every one and a level-0 antichain of 6 or 7 independent tasks. THE PLAN CAN ALREADY FILL THE FLEET. Suppressing over-split is NOT what starves it, so converge_off would have burned a cell to move a number that was never the problem. WHAT THE IDLE ACTUALLY IS, MEASURED BY SAMPLING THE DISPATCH WINDOW EVERY 5 SECONDS AND ATTRIBUTING EVERY SOLO INTERVAL TO THE TASK HOLDING IT: baseline-n3-r0 has 73 minutes of solo time, 49 percent of the window, and integrate-verify owns 71.4 of those minutes, NINETY-EIGHT PERCENT. baseline-n3-r1 has 61 minutes solo, 55 percent, and integrate-verify owns 53.5, EIGHTY-EIGHT PERCENT. baseline-n3-r2 has only 10 minutes solo, 16 percent, and integrate-verify owns NONE of it - that cell's sink did not run long, which is exactly why its occupancy was the best of the three and why its wall was 85 minutes. SO THE OCCUPANCY COLLAPSE IS NOT A WIDTH PROBLEM AND NEVER WAS. IT IS ONE TERMINAL TASK HOLDING THE WHOLE FLEET FOR HALF THE RUN, WHICH IS PRECISELY F454's AMDAHL DIAGNOSIS ARRIVING FROM A SECOND, INDEPENDENT DIRECTION. F455 sharded the e2e half of the join and that half is now genuinely parallel - verify-e2e::2 accounts for only 1.4 and 7.5 minutes of solo time - but integrate-verify itself remains ONE task, the sole repair point, and its cost scales with the tree the fleet just produced. THE NEXT EXPERIMENT IS THEREFORE complete_parallel, GOOSE_SWARM_COMPLETE_PARALLEL, WHICH FANS THE REPAIR BY FAILING FILE, AND NOT converge_off AND NOT spec_sized_plan. Both of those now look like they move numbers that are not on the critical path. ⚠️ SEVENTH BLIND ZERO ON THE WAY HERE, AND IT WOULD HAVE BEEN THE WORST ONE YET: my first width probe read t.get depends_on, which does not exist - the field is deps - so EVERY plan came back width equals all tasks and depth 1, i.e. PERFECTLY PARALLEL. I would have published that the DAG has no dependencies at all, which flatly contradicts the 49 percent solo time I had just measured. THE CONTRADICTION IS WHAT SAVED ME, not diligence. I then added positive controls, a hand-built chain that must report width 1 and a hand-built fan that must report 3, and both passed before I believed any real number. ⇒ L272 COMPUTE THE CHEAP DIAGNOSTIC BEFORE BUYING THE EXPENSIVE EXPERIMENT - A PLAN-SHAPE QUESTION ANSWERABLE FROM AN EXISTING LOG SHOULD NEVER COST A FLEET CELL, AND WHEN A NEW NUMBER CONTRADICTS ONE YOU ALREADY TRUST, SUSPECT THE NEW PROBE FIRST
F467|THE ARM I BUILT MEASURES THE WRONG QUANTITY - MODULE COUNT DOES NOT PREDICT TREE SIZE, AND TREE SIZE IS WHAT DRIVES THE SINK|Chasing why baseline-n3-r2 was the best 3-node cell on every measure at once - score 0.9343, the shortest wall at 85 minutes, the best occupancy, and only 16 percent solo time - I found the sink table. SINK DURATION AND ATTEMPTS AGAINST TREE BYTES: n3-r0 60732 bytes gave 71.4 minutes over 2 attempts, n3-r1 58630 gave 53.6 over 3, n3-r2 31900 gave 31.9 over 1, n1-r0 37037 gave 19.3 over 1, n1-r1 35847 gave 35.9 over 1. THE ATTEMPT COUNT SPLITS CLEANLY WITH NO OVERLAP: every tree at or above 58k needed 2 or 3 sink attempts, every tree at or below 37k needed exactly 1. A retry re-runs the join, so the retry is the multiplier and a big tree is what buys it. I EXPECTED THIS TO VINDICATE THE spec_sized_plan ARM - smaller plan, smaller tree, shorter sink - AND IT DOES THE OPPOSITE. n3-r1 AND n3-r2 BOTH EMITTED EXACTLY 6 MODULES, and they produced 11 files and 58630 bytes versus 8 files and 31900 bytes. SAME MODULE COUNT, TWICE THE TREE. Module count therefore does not predict tree size, tree size is what predicts sink attempts, and the arm's registered threshold is a MODULE COUNT - so it is measuring a quantity that does not move the thing that matters. That is now the THIRD independent reason to doubt an arm I shipped six hours ago: F465 said it cuts width on a starved fleet, F466 said width was never the constraint, and this says its own metric is uncorrelated with the outcome. THE THRESHOLD STILL DOES NOT MOVE and the arm still runs if the sweep reaches it, but I am recording NOW, before it runs, that I no longer expect it to matter and that a pass on it would not be a win. WHAT ACTUALLY DRIVES TREE BYTES IS FILES AND CODE PER FILE, WHICH IS MODEL VERBOSITY RATHER THAN PLAN SHAPE, and I have no lever on it. THE LEVER I DO HAVE IS THE RETRY: the sink re-runs the ENTIRE join on failure, so a 58k tree pays the join two or three times while a 32k tree pays it once. ALSO CONFIRMED BY READING RATHER THAN ASSUMING, PER L272: complete_parallel fans the push-to-completion FIX step, a phase AFTER execute that uses complete_verify and smoke_fix_target. IT DOES NOT TOUCH integrate-verify. NO EXISTING LEVER FANS THE SINK. The sink's own work is 22 shell plus 4 write plus 2 edit calls, so it both runs and repairs, serially, alone, for half the run. ⇒ L273 WHEN A NATURAL EXPERIMENT ALREADY RAN, MINE IT BEFORE COMMISSIONING A NEW ONE - THE BEST CELL OF THE NIGHT WAS SITTING IN THE RESULTS DIRECTORY AND IT FALSIFIED THE ARM I HAD BUILT TO PRODUCE IT
F468|THE SINK'S RETRIES ARE MID-STREAM CONNECTION DROPS, NOT TIMEOUTS - A TRANSIENT NETWORK FAULT THROWS AWAY THE LONGEST TASK IN THE RUN AND RESTARTS IT FROM ZERO|I hypothesised that the baked 900s worker_timeout_secs was killing the sink's first attempt, because n3-r0's first attempt ended at 916 seconds, which is 900 plus change. THAT HYPOTHESIS IS REFUTED AND I REFUTED IT BEFORE BUILDING ANYTHING ON IT. It was already only 1 for 2 on timing - n3-r1's first attempt ended at 797s and its second at 1860s, neither of which is 900 - so instead of inferring a cause from a clock I dumped the retry event and READ IT. THE ACTUAL ERRORS: n3-r0 retry says stream decode error, mid-stream body drop, on integrate-verify, transient true. n3-r1 first retry says agent stalled, no progress for 420s, no token or tool activity. n3-r1 second retry says stream decode error, mid-stream body drop, again. SO TWO OF THE THREE SINK RETRIES IN THIS CAMPAIGN ARE MID-STREAM CONNECTION DROPS AND THE THIRD IS THE 420-SECOND PROGRESS WATCHDOG. NONE IS THE WORKER TIMEOUT. THIS IS A BETTER DIAGNOSIS THAN THE ONE IT REPLACES AND IT REFRAMES F467. Tree size does not cause retries directly - it causes them by EXPOSURE. The sink is the single longest generation in the run, a bigger tree makes it longer still, and a longer generation is simply more time during which the LM Studio stream can drop. That is why attempts split so cleanly at 58k versus 37k without tree size being mechanically responsible for anything. THE COST IS NOT SMALL AND IT IS PURE WASTE: n3-r0 threw away 916 seconds, 15.3 minutes, or 7.9 percent of its 193-minute wall. n3-r1 threw away 797 plus 1860 equals 2657 seconds, 44.3 minutes, TWENTY-NINE POINT FIVE PERCENT OF ITS 150-MINUTE WALL. Both drops occurred on different devices, worksmacstudio and local-mihai, so this is not one bad node. AND THE STRUCTURAL PROBLEM IS THE RECOVERY, NOT THE FAULT. A dropped stream is transient and unavoidable on a LAN fleet; what makes it expensive is that the engine's response is to re-dispatch the WHOLE integrate-verify task to another node and start the join again from nothing, discarding every command already run and every fix already written. The longest, most serial, most fleet-blocking task in the run has the LEAST resilient failure mode. ⇒ THE ENGINE CHANGE THIS POINTS AT IS TO MAKE A TRANSIENT STREAM FAULT NOT COST THE ENTIRE JOIN - retry the STREAM rather than the TASK, or checkpoint the sink so a restart resumes instead of restarting. PREDICTION, ONE THRESHOLD, REGISTERED NOW: across the next five 3-node cells, at least one sink retry will again carry a transient error of stream decode or agent stalled rather than a worker timeout. FALSIFIED IF ZERO OF THEM DO. ⇒ L274 A CLOCK THAT MATCHES A CONFIGURED LIMIT IS A COINCIDENCE UNTIL THE ERROR STRING AGREES - THE EVENT CARRIED THE REASON ALL ALONG AND I WAS ABOUT TO INFER A DIFFERENT ONE FROM ARITHMETIC
F469|MY LOOP DIED FOR SEVEN HOURS AND THE ONLY REASON THE NIGHT WAS NOT LOST IS THAT THE WORK WAS NOT IN THE LOOP|I failed to re-arm ScheduleWakeup as the first action of a turn and the tick simply stopped. From 09:40 to 16:41 there were NO wakeups, and I did not notice - MIHAI DID, and asked. Seven hours of an unattended run in which the supervising agent was absent. THE SWEEP SURVIVED COMPLETELY INTACT: pid 41030 with ppid 1 kept running the whole time and finished THREE more cells, baseline-n3-r0 at 10:40, baseline-n1-r0 at 12:30 and baseline-n3-r1 at 14:51, and was already 2 hours into the next one. THAT IS THE ENTIRE ARGUMENT FOR THE LAYER-A DESIGN, DEMONSTRATED AT MY OWN EXPENSE: the part that lived in a detached process kept producing evidence, and the part that lived in the conversation evaporated without a trace or a warning. A loop that only reports good news is indistinguishable from a loop that died in minute three, and mine was indistinguishable from one that died in hour one. GOAL STATUS AS OF 16:41, AND IT MOVED. 1-node n equals 2: mean score 0.7113, mean wall 112 minutes, EXECUTE OCCUPANCY 1.0000 and 1.0000. 3-node n equals 3: mean score 0.8557, mean wall 135 minutes, EXECUTE OCCUPANCY 0.5535, 0.6678 and 0.8256. SO THREE NODES NOW SCORE PLUS 0.1444 AND THE WALL GAP HAS NARROWED FROM 1.63x TO 1.21x. ⚠️ THAT IS NOT A WIN AND I WILL NOT REPORT IT AS ONE. The 3-node score spread alone runs 0.7151 to 0.9343, so a plus 0.1444 mean difference sits INSIDE the noise of a single arm, and TWO OF THE FIVE CELLS RAN ON THE OLDER BINARY 235858848 while three ran on 235858816 - a MIXED-BUILD table is not a clean comparison and the instrument-versioning rule says so explicitly. THE ONE GENUINELY NEW SIGNAL IS baseline-n3-r1 AT EXECUTE OCCUPANCY 0.8256, well clear of the prior 3-node band of 0.5535 to 0.6678 and the first 3-node cell ever to get off the occupancy floor. IT IS ONE CELL AND I CANNOT ATTRIBUTE IT: the e2e-oracle fix is a candidate, so is ordinary variance, and the sink-retry fixes are NOT in that binary at all. ⇒ L275 PUT THE WORK IN A PROCESS AND THE JUDGEMENT IN THE TICK, NEVER THE REVERSE - WHEN THE TICK DIES, ANYTHING THAT LIVED THERE DIES SILENTLY, AND SILENCE IS THE ONE FAILURE MODE THAT LOOKS EXACTLY LIKE SUCCESS
F470|REGISTERED BEFORE IT RUNS - THE JUDGE NOW HAS A VERDICT IT CAN REACH FOR THE SINK, AND HERE IS THE ONE THING THAT WOULD PROVE IT|Mihai asked why the judge is not dealing with the sink. THE ANSWER IS THAT IT COULD NOT: every deterministic gate in judge.rs tests owned_files is not empty, the Accept branch additionally requires all_owned_present, and Accept and Looping exclude integrate-verify BY NAME on top of that, and even secs_since_last_write is derived only from owned files so it is None for a task that owns none. integrate-verify owns nothing, so NOT ONE VERDICT WAS REACHABLE FOR IT, and the scheduler's own comment states the consequence in plain words - leave it to worker_timeout as the hard-stall backstop. A timer spoke because the judge had been left mute. THE WAY IN WAS is_still_producing, the single predicate that reads TOOL CALLS from the activity digest rather than files on disk, so it works on a task with no artifacts at all. Shipped in 3ca937172: an owns-nothing Accept branch gated on having acted at least once, being at least min_age_secs.max(420) old, and no longer producing; plus the scheduler no longer judges an owns-nothing task once-and-never-again, which alone would have made the branch unreachable because a first pass early in a join is always too young for it. PREDICTION, ONE THRESHOLD, AND THE INERT CASE IS NAMED SO IT CANNOT BE CLAIMED AS A PASS: among the next 3-node cells in which the sink GOES QUIET - no tool-call growth across a judge observation gap - for at least 420 seconds, AT LEAST ONE judge_verdict event must appear with task_id integrate-verify and verdict accept. FALSIFIED if such a quiet sink occurs and no accept verdict does, which would mean the branch is unreachable in practice for a reason I have not found. INERT, AND EXPLICITLY NOT A PASS, if no sink ever goes quiet in those cells - the lever would simply never have had a precondition, which says nothing about whether it works. WHY I EXPECT THIS TO BE MODEST RATHER THAN