F471|THE JOIN'S TIME IS NOT COMMANDS AND NOT OBVIOUSLY GENERATION EITHER - TWO CANDIDATE FIXES REFUTED FOR FREE BEFORE EITHER COST A CELL|I was going to fan the sink's REPAIR by failing file, copying the pattern complete_parallel uses for the post-execute phase. THE CHEAP DIAGNOSTIC KILLED IT FIRST. The join's own activity digest across four cells: baseline-n3-r0 ran 21 tool calls and 25132 thinking chars in a 71.4-minute sink; n3-r1 ran 11 calls and 7387 chars in 53.6 minutes; n3-r2 ran 29 calls and 13030 chars in 31.9 minutes; n1-r0 ran 17 calls and 8258 chars in 19.3 minutes. ELEVEN TOOL CALLS CANNOT FILL FIFTY-THREE MINUTES, so the cost is not command execution and fanning the commands would have bought almost nothing. AND TOOL CALLS DO NOT PREDICT DURATION - THEY RUN BACKWARDS: the cell with the MOST calls, 29, had the SHORTEST sink at 31.9 minutes, and the cell with the FEWEST, 11, ran 53.6. Thinking chars do not predict it cleanly either - 25132 gave the longest sink and 8258 the shortest, which fits, but 7387 gave 53.6 minutes while 13030 gave 31.9, which does not. SO NEITHER ACTIONS NOR GENERATED TOKENS EXPLAIN THE JOIN'S WALL, AND I AM NOT GOING TO INVENT A THIRD EXPLANATION AND ACT ON IT. The remaining suspect is PREFILL - the join reads the whole assembled tree plus every shard's report, and `lms ps` shows nodes sitting in PROCESSINGPROMPT, which is prefill and not generation - but I have measured nothing that separates prefill from idle waiting, so this is a HYPOTHESIS AND NOT A FINDING. SECOND CANDIDATE REFUTED IN THE SAME PASS, AND THIS ONE IS EMBARRASSING: I was about to propose `sink_lean_prefill` as the fix, since its doc says the contract bundle is dead context that slows the sink's prefill and the sink is the slowest task. IT IS ALREADY BAKED ON - `Default for SwarmConfig` sets `Some(true)` and the source carries a comment reading IT IS ALREADY ON in capitals, put there precisely because I PUBLISHED A FALSE CLAIM ABOUT THIS LEVER ONCE BEFORE, which line 5146 records. THAT WOULD HAVE BEEN THE FOURTH TIME TODAY I REACHED FOR SOMETHING THE REPOSITORY ALREADY HAD, AND THE SECOND TIME ON THIS EXACT LEVER. ⇒ L277 WHEN A DIAGNOSTIC REFUTES YOUR PLANNED FIX, THAT IS THE DIAGNOSTIC EARNING ITS KEEP, NOT A SETBACK - TWO EXPENSIVE EXPERIMENTS DIED HERE FOR THE COST OF READING FOUR JSON FILES, AND THE HONEST NEXT STEP IS TO MEASURE WHERE THE JOIN'S SECONDS ACTUALLY GO BEFORE PROPOSING ANOTHER FIX FOR THEM
F472|JOIN DURATION DRIVES OCCUPANCY - AND THE SAME MEASUREMENT MADE ME REVERT MY OWN FIX ONE HOUR AFTER SHIPPING IT|Splitting every sink's wall into DISCARDED versus PRODUCTIVE attempts is the sharpest thing measured today. baseline-n3-r1 ran ONE attempt of 480 seconds with no retries and scored EXECUTE OCCUPANCY 0.8256, the best 3-node cell on record. baseline-n3-r0 ran THREE attempts - 1383s discarded, 941s discarded, 3135s completed, 5459s total with 43 PERCENT THROWN AWAY - and scored 0.5535. SAME ARM, SAME BINARY, SAME SPEC: THE ENTIRE DIFFERENCE IS THE JOIN. That closes the loop opened by F465 and F466, which showed occupancy separates the arms and that the idle is one task, and it now says WHICH property of that task matters. It also explains n3-r1's outlier occupancy, which I had recorded as unexplained. AND IT REFUTED MY OWN SHIPPED FIX. 62988a0d0 capped an owns-nothing task at min of max_attempts and 2, on the premise that a task which cannot resume loses everything on each restart so past one retry every further attempt is pure waste. IN n3-r0 THE THIRD ATTEMPT IS THE ONE THAT COMPLETED AND DID THE REPAIR WORK. The cap would have hit exhaustion after the two dead attempts and degraded the join, discarding the attempt that actually succeeded - converting a completed join into an unfinished one to save time that was not the problem in that cell. REVERTED IN 3d160cb2a, ONE HOUR AFTER SHIPPING. The degrade-at-exhaustion path from db54b8802 is untouched and still correct; capping the budget BEFORE exhaustion is a separate claim and the evidence does not support it. THE OTHER HALF IS THAT THE JOIN'S SECONDS ARE NOT WHERE I THOUGHT. n3-r1's completing session shows 465 seconds of LLM turn time inside a 480-second attempt - NINETY-SEVEN PERCENT ACCOUNTED, nothing pathological, no prefill pathology, no slow turn. A long join is long because ATTEMPTS GET DISCARDED, not because any single turn is slow. So the prefill hypothesis from F471 is DEAD TOO, and I never spent a cell on it. ⚠️ THE TRAP THAT NEARLY RUINED THIS: CELL NAMES ARE REUSED. The sweep re-ran baseline-n3-r0 and n3-r1 at 10:40 and 14:51, so today's logs are NOT the runs F468 analysed, and for several minutes I had a retry timeline from one generation sitting beside a session from another and drew a conclusion from the pair. The numbers disagreeing is what caught it. ALWAYS check a cell's result mtime and engine_build before comparing across analyses. ⇒ L278 THE FASTEST WAY TO A REAL CAUSE IS TO SPLIT A DURATION INTO THE PART THAT SURVIVED AND THE PART THAT WAS THROWN AWAY - A MEAN HIDES A RETRY, AND A RETRY IS NOT SLOWNESS, IT IS DELETION
F473|F468'S PREDICTION IS CONFIRMED THREE FOR THREE - EVERY SINK DISCARD IN THIS GENERATION IS A MID-STREAM BODY DROP, AND THE ENGINE THROWS AWAY AN HOUR BECAUSE IT CANNOT RESUME A CONVERSATION|F468 registered, before the outcome, that across the next 3-node cells at least one sink retry would carry a transient stream-decode or agent-stalled error rather than a worker timeout, and that it would be FALSIFIED if zero did. READ FROM THE CURRENT GENERATION'S LOGS: baseline-n3-r0 has TWO integrate-verify retries, both stream decode error, mid-stream body drop. baseline-n1-r0 has ONE, the same. baseline-n3-r1's only retry is on verify::store and is a 420s stall - AND ITS SINK HAD ZERO RETRIES, WHICH IS EXACTLY WHY THAT CELL RAN A 480-SECOND JOIN AND SCORED THE BEST OCCUPANCY ON RECORD. SO EVERY SINK DISCARD IN THIS GENERATION IS A DROPPED HTTP BODY. THREE FOR THREE. NOT ONE IS A TIMEOUT. THE PREDICTION IS CONFIRMED AND THE CAUSE IS NO LONGER IN DOUBT. THE MECHANISM, READ IN SOURCE RATHER THAN INFERRED. A fleet node drops the HTTP response body mid-generation; the provider raises NetworkError; the agent reply loop yields it as assistant TEXT and BREAKS, so run_agent_in returns Ok with a TRUNCATED verdict. swarm.rs detects that signature deliberately - it was added to stop a SILENT FALSE-GREEN where a truncated verify was accepted as done - and returns Transient, and the scheduler re-dispatches THE WHOLE TASK. The detection is correct and necessary. THE COST IS ENTIRELY IN THE RECOVERY. WHY THE RECOVERY IS SO EXPENSIVE, AND IT IS A CONTRACT PROBLEM NOT A BUG: run_agent_in takes a system_prompt and a user_text and ALWAYS STARTS A FRESH CONVERSATION. There is no parameter for a prior session. Resume exists in this engine only at RUN level, resume_state_from_log, never at TASK level. So a body dropped at minute 23 of a join discards twenty-three minutes of established context - what it had already read, correlated and decided - even though every message up to the drop is sitting in the sessions DB and every file edit it had already made is still on disk. THE WORK SURVIVES; THE UNDERSTANDING DOES NOT. I AM NOT BUILDING SESSION-RESUME BLIND. Threading a prior session into run_agent_in changes that function's contract and may reach goose core, which the operating scope forbids me to edit for a swarm problem. THE SMALLER IN-SCOPE CANDIDATE, AND I AM NAMING IT BEFORE MEASURING SO IT CANNOT BE RETROFITTED: a ContentRetry threads its error into the next attempt's prior_hint so the retry is GUIDED, while an infra Transient does not - the code says so explicitly. A stream-drop retry on a join is therefore a BLIND RE-ROLL that redoes the analysis from nothing. Giving it a prior_hint that names what the interrupted attempt already did is cheap, is entirely inside swarm.rs, and does not touch the detection that prevents the false-green. ⇒ L279 WHEN A FAULT IS UNAVOIDABLE, THE ONLY LEVER IS THE COST OF RECOVERY - AND THE ENGINE THAT CANNOT RESUME PAYS FULL PRICE EVERY TIME FOR A FAULT IT DID NOT CAUSE
F474|THE RETRY THAT PAYS FOR THE DROP NOW KNOWS THE DROP HAPPENED - REGISTERED BEFORE IT RUNS, WITH ITS INERT CASE NAMED|F473 established beyond doubt that every sink discard in this generation is a mid-stream body drop, three for three, and that the detection which converts it into a retry is CORRECT and must not be weakened because it is what stops a truncated verify being accepted as a silent false green. The cost is entirely in the recovery, and the recovery is expensive for a contract reason: run_agent_in takes a system prompt and a user text and ALWAYS starts a fresh conversation, so the retry begins with no memory of the twenty-three minutes the dropped attempt had already spent. Every message is in the sessions DB and every file edit is on disk, but the new conversation knows about none of it. THE WORK SURVIVES AND THE UNDERSTANDING DOES NOT. SHIPPED, AND IT IS DELIBERATELY THE SMALL VERSION. The scheduler already threads a guided note into the next attempt for a CONTENT retry, and deliberately withholds one from infra transients, with a comment explaining why - a model-unloaded retry means NOTHING HAPPENED and a stale note would mislead the worker. THAT REASONING IS RIGHT FOR EVERY INFRA TRANSIENT EXCEPT ONE. A dropped body means the opposite of nothing happening: the worker was mid-generation, producing, and its earlier tool calls already landed. So transient_retry_hint gives exactly that case a note telling the fresh conversation that it was cut off by a dropped connection rather than rejected, that anything it had written or edited IS STILL ON DISK, and that it must read the current state and continue rather than re-derive from scratch. Extracted as a pure function so it is unit-testable without a live scheduler, the same way should_degrade_on_stall was, and the test pins BOTH directions - a dropped body earns a hint, while model is not loaded, connection reset, 529 Overloaded, invalid model identifier and the empty string all stay silent. PREDICTION, ONE THRESHOLD, INERT CASE NAMED SO IT CANNOT BE CLAIMED AS A PASS: in a cell where integrate-verify suffers a mid-stream body drop and is re-dispatched, the SURVIVING attempt after the drop completes in LESS wall-clock than the attempt that was dropped. FALSIFIED if it takes as long or longer, which would mean the hint changed nothing and the retry re-derived everything anyway. INERT, AND EXPLICITLY NOT A PASS, if no sink suffers a drop in those cells. ⚠️ CONFIDENCE IS DELIBERATELY MODEST AND I AM RECORDING IT NOW: this is a PROMPT crossing a boundary that a resumed SESSION would cross properly, and a 27B model may simply ignore it. It cannot make things worse - the alternative is a blind re-roll - but a hint is not a resume, and if this fails the honest conclusion is that per-task session resume is the real fix and belongs on the list rather than another prompt. ⇒ L280 WHEN THE FIX YOU CAN REACH IS SMALLER THAN THE FIX THAT IS RIGHT, SHIP THE SMALL ONE AND WRITE DOWN THAT IT IS THE SMALL ONE - OTHERWISE ITS FAILURE WILL BE READ AS THE BIG ONE FAILING
F475|SCOPE CORRECTION BEFORE IT HARDENS - THE DROPS HIT BOTH ARMS, SO THEY EXPLAIN WASTE BUT NOT THE THREE-VERSUS-ONE GAP|F473 confirmed that every sink discard is a mid-stream body drop and I was one write-up away from letting that become the explanation for why three nodes lose to one. IT IS NOT, AND THE TALLY SAYS SO PLAINLY. Sink drops and discarded seconds per cell: baseline-n1-r0 ONE drop, 1031s discarded of a 1724s sink; baseline-n1-r1 zero drops, 2151s sink; baseline-n3-r0 TWO drops, 2324s discarded of 5459s; baseline-n3-r1 zero drops, 480s sink; baseline-n3-r2 zero drops, 1914s sink; the live swarm-1node-r1 ONE drop, 1949s discarded. SO DROPS LAND ON ONE-NODE CELLS TOO - two of the three 1-node cells on record dropped, against one of three 3-node cells. THE DROP IS A PROPERTY OF THE TRANSPORT AND THE LENGTH OF THE GENERATION, NOT OF THE FLEET SIZE. What drops explain is VARIANCE WITHIN an arm, and they explain it very well: the 3-node cell that dropped nothing ran a 480-second join and posted 0.8256 occupancy, the best on record, while the one that dropped twice ran 5459 seconds and posted 0.5535. That is the whole spread of the 3-node arm, and it is also why F461's score spread of 0.1531 was so wide. WHAT THIS DOES NOT EXPLAIN IS WHY THE 3-NODE ARM IS SLOWER ON AVERAGE, and I have to keep those two claims apart. A fix that eliminates drops would tighten BOTH arms and shrink the noise that has made every comparison in this campaign uninterpretable - which is worth a great deal on its own, since F461 and F462 both died on that noise - but it would not by itself make three nodes beat one. THE 3-VERSUS-1 GAP STILL NEEDS ITS OWN CAUSE, AND THE CANDIDATE REMAINS F454's: THE 3-NODE ARM BUILDS A 1.6x LARGER TREE AND THE SERIAL JOIN MUST SWALLOW IT. ⚠️ I AM REGISTERING THIS BEFORE ANY F474 RESULT ARRIVES, because a drop-related win would be very easy to narrate as a node-curve win and it would not be one. ⇒ L281 A CAUSE THAT AFFLICTS BOTH ARMS EXPLAINS THE NOISE, NEVER THE DIFFERENCE - CHECK WHICH ARMS A MECHANISM TOUCHES BEFORE YOU LET IT EXPLAIN A COMPARISON
F476|THE WALL GAP HAS CLOSED TO PARITY - AND BOTH MECHANISMS I PROPOSED FOR IT FAIL TO REPRODUCE ON THE CURRENT GENERATION|baseline-n1-r1 landed at 17:30 and the table is now nearly single-build, four of five cells on 235858816. ONE NODE n equals 2: score 0.6355, wall 134 minutes, EXECUTE OCCUPANCY 1.0000. THREE NODES n equals 3: score 0.8557, wall 135 minutes, occupancy 0.6823. THE 1.63x WALL GAP OF F454 IS GONE - 134 AGAINST 135 MINUTES IS PARITY - AND THE 3-NODE ARM SCORES 0.2202 HIGHER. THAT IS THE CLOSEST THIS CAMPAIGN HAS COME TO THE GOAL. I AM NOT CALLING IT A WIN AND HERE IS EXACTLY WHY. FIRST, THE 1-NODE WALL IS INFLATED BY NOISE, NOT BY A PROPERTY: baseline-n1-r1 ran 159 minutes and its sink lost 1949 seconds, 32 minutes, to a single mid-stream body drop. Remove that one transport fault and the 1-node mean falls back near 118 minutes and the parity evaporates. F475 predicted exactly this hazard - drops hit both arms, so they move whichever mean happens to catch one. SECOND, THE SCORE DELTA IS STILL NOT CLEAR OF NOISE: plus 0.2202 against a within-3-node spread of 0.2192, from 0.7151 to 0.9343. The gap and the noise are the same size. THIRD, OCCUPANCY STILL SEPARATES THE ARMS COMPLETELY AND STILL SAYS THE FLEET IS WASTED: 1.0000 against 0.6823, no overlap, five cells deep. A THREE-NODE FLEET IS STILL IDLE ROUGHLY A THIRD OF ITS EXECUTE WINDOW. AND BOTH MECHANISMS I OFFERED FOR THE GAP FAIL ON THIS GENERATION. F457 claimed the fleet-scaled ask inflates the plan, measured as 1-node 5 and 5 modules against 3-node 7 and 6. TODAY: 1-node emits 4 and 6, 3-node emits 6, 6 and 6. THE ARMS NOW OVERLAP - one 1-node cell emitted exactly as many modules as every 3-node cell - so the module-count difference DOES NOT REPRODUCE. F454 claimed a 1.6x tree ratio, 36.4k against 59.7k. TODAY the first sink tree per cell reads 34502 and 41353 for 1-node against 52343, 64090 and 31900 for 3-node: a ratio of about 1.30x, AND baseline-n3-r2 at 31900 bytes is SMALLER THAN BOTH 1-NODE CELLS. The separation is gone. SO THE HONEST POSITION IS THAT THE 3-VERSUS-1 WALL GAP HAS LARGELY VANISHED AND NEITHER OF MY EXPLANATIONS FOR IT SURVIVES CONTACT WITH THE CURRENT DATA. I would rather record that than keep a tidy story: F454 and F457 were measured on a generation that has since been re-run, and CELL NAMES ARE REUSED, which is the same trap that has now cost me three separate re-checks today. WHAT REMAINS SOLID IS THE OCCUPANCY DEFICIT, WHICH IS THE ONE THING THAT HAS NEVER WOBBLED ACROSS ANY GENERATION OR BINARY. ⇒ L282 WHEN THE EFFECT YOU SET OUT TO EXPLAIN DISAPPEARS, SAY SO BEFORE YOU SAY ANYTHING ABOUT YOUR EXPLANATIONS - AN OBSOLETE MECHANISM FOR A VANISHED EFFECT IS TWO ERRORS THAT LOOK LIKE ONE FINDING
F477|I PUBLISHED A FALSE CLAIM IN d04d0e8de - THE DESKTOP ALWAYS HAD A JOIN CAP, AND I FOUND OUT ONLY BECAUSE I WENT LOOKING FOR A DIFFERENT LEVER|While checking whether sink_review could be made config-reachable I grepped for set_var precedent and found run_swarm already bridging cfg.sink_cap_secs into GOOSE_SWARM_SINK_CAP_SECS whenever the env is unset. THE BRIDGE'S OWN COMMENT SAYS IT IS THE ONLY THING THAT CARRIES THE BAKED CEILING TO A DESKTOP RUN, and it says so because a previous reader corrected exactly this misreading before me. SO MY CLAIM IN d04d0e8de - that the read was env-only and therefore the desktop had NO join cap at all - IS FALSE. The ceiling was always there. The code change itself is harmless and mildly useful: the read site now resolves env then config directly instead of DEPENDING ON THE BRIDGE HAVING RUN FIRST, which removes an ordering coupling. But the JUSTIFICATION was wrong, and it went out in a commit message and to Mihai before I checked. THAT IS L258 EXACTLY - THE RIGHT CHANGE FOR THE WRONG REASON LEAVES THE REASON IN THE RECORD - and it is the second false claim I have shipped today after F450's gigabytes-as-tokens-per-second. I have rewritten the source comment to state the correction rather than the falsehood, because this codebase carries several comments whose whole purpose is to record that an earlier comment lied, and mine was about to join them uncorrected. THE INVARIANT TEST STANDS: sink_cap_secs greater than zero is still worth asserting beside complete_cap_secs, since a zero would mean an unbounded join on any surface, and that is true independent of how the value reaches the read site. WHAT I WAS ACTUALLY LOOKING FOR IS STILL REAL AND IS NOW THE LEAD CANDIDATE. sink_review is the idle-fill mechanism that gives free nodes read-only whole-tree review work WHILE THE JOIN RUNS - precisely the occupancy deficit that is the only measure never to have wobbled, 1.0000 against 0.6823 across five cells with no overlap. sink_review_enabled reads the environment ONLY, unwrap_or false, with NO config bridge of the kind sink_cap_secs has, and levers.sink_review is ABSENT from every cell I checked. IT HAS NEVER RUN. ⚠️ AND IT IS DELICATE: the source states that both halves of the mechanism, the producer in goose-swarm and the drain in goose-cli, MUST READ THE SAME ANSWER or the run reports a lever it is not running - which is why a single env read exists. Any config path must preserve that single resolution, and the safe shape is the bridge pattern already used above, not a second independent read. ⇒ L283 BEFORE CLAIMING A VALUE NEVER REACHES ITS READ SITE, GREP FOR A BRIDGE - A LEVER CAN BE CONFIG-REACHABLE THROUGH A WRITE YOU DID NOT LOOK FOR
F478|THE ONE MECHANISM AIMED AT THE ONLY SURVIVING DEFICIT HAS NEVER RUN ANYWHERE - NOW IT IS REACHABLE, AND STILL OFF|Every candidate explanation for the node curve has now failed to reproduce: F457's plan inflation is gone, today's 1-node cells emit 4 and 6 modules against 3-node's 6, 6 and 6, and F454's 1.6x tree ratio is down to about 1.30x with baseline-n3-r2 SMALLER than both 1-node cells. Even the wall gap itself has closed to 134 against 135 minutes. ONE MEASUREMENT HAS NEVER WOBBLED ACROSS ANY GENERATION OR ANY BINARY: EXECUTE OCCUPANCY IS 1.0000 ON BOTH 1-NODE CELLS AND 0.5535, 0.6678, 0.8256 ON THE THREE 3-NODE CELLS. Five cells, no overlap. A three-node fleet spends roughly a third of its execute window idle, and the idle is dominated by the join running alone - 100 percent of the solo time in the worst cell, re-verified on the CURRENT generation rather than inherited from the re-run one. SO THE DEFICIT IS THE TARGET, AND sink_review IS THE ONE MECHANISM IN THE ENGINE AIMED SQUARELY AT IT: while integrate-verify runs, hand the FREE nodes read-only whole-tree review work and give its findings to the join. IT HAS NEVER RUN ANYWHERE. sink_review_enabled reads the ENVIRONMENT ONLY with unwrap_or false, and unlike sink_cap_secs it had NO bridge carrying a config value to it, so levers.sink_review is ABSENT from every cell on record and a desktop launch - which cannot set environment variables at all - could never have switched it on. SHIPPED AS REACHABILITY, NOT AS A DEFAULT, AND THE DISTINCTION IS THE POINT. A config field plus a bridge in run_swarm, defaulting None which is OFF and byte-identical: only a config value of TRUE writes the variable, so an unset or false config leaves the OFF path exactly as it was. THE BRIDGE SHAPE IS DELIBERATE AND NOT THE OBVIOUS ONE - the source states that both halves of this mechanism, the producer in goose-swarm and the drain in goose-cli, MUST read the same answer or the run reports a lever it is not running, so I wrote the env it already reads rather than adding a second independently-resolved read. A second read would have been simpler and would have quietly broken the invariant the comment exists to protect. PREDICTION, ONE THRESHOLD, INERT CASE NAMED: a 3-node cell with sink_review ON reaches EXECUTE OCCUPANCY ABOVE 0.6823, the current 3-node arm mean. FALSIFIED at or below it. INERT AND EXPLICITLY NOT A PASS if armcheck reports BLOCKED because the sink never ran ALONE in that cell - with no solo window there is no idle to fill and the arm proves nothing either way. ⚠️ AND THE HONEST CAVEAT: FILLING IDLE NODES RAISES OCCUPANCY BY CONSTRUCTION. If the review findings do not actually help the join, this buys a better-looking number and no better app - so the arm must be judged on SCORE AND WALL TOO, never on occupancy alone, which is exactly the trap of optimising the instrument instead of the outcome. ⇒ L284 MAKING A LEVER REACHABLE AND TURNING IT ON ARE TWO DIFFERENT CHANGES - SHIP THE FIRST AS A DEFECT FIX AND MAKE THE SECOND EARN ITS EVIDENCE
