F481|SLOT UTILISATION IS NOT MEASURABLE AGAINST WEIGHT BECAUSE WEIGHT IS NOT A HARD CAP - THE FLEET RUNS MORE CONCURRENT TASKS THAN THE POOL DECLARES|F480 named an unmeasured gap: device-busy occupancy says nothing about whether a busy machine had both its slots full, so I set out to measure slot utilisation as task-seconds over exec_wall times slots. THE POSITIVE CONTROL CAUGHT IT IMMEDIATELY: baseline-n1-r1 returned 1.0047, and a utilisation above 1.0 is impossible. I first assumed my probe was double-counting retried tasks, which is exactly the failure occupancy.py documents having hit once at 1.118, so I unioned each task's spans before summing. THE NUMBER DID NOT MOVE. The probe was not the problem. THE DENOMINATOR WAS. baseline-n1-r1 declares ONE device at WEIGHT 2, every dispatch went to that one device, and PEAK CONCURRENCY WAS THREE TASKS. The three co-running at the peak were verify-e2e::0, verify-e2e::1 and test-api - TWO owns-nothing read-only e2e shards plus one file-owning task. That is consistent with owns-nothing verification tasks co-running OUTSIDE the per-device weight accounting, which would be a deliberate design rather than a defect: fan_e2e_split's own comment says the shards own no files SO THEY CAN CO-RUN RACE-FREE. I am stating that as CONSISTENT WITH, not proven - I have one cell's peak, not the dispatch rule. WHAT FOLLOWS IS DEFINITE THOUGH. SLOT UTILISATION AGAINST DECLARED WEIGHT IS ILL-DEFINED AND I WILL NOT REPORT IT. A ratio whose denominator the engine is free to exceed is not a utilisation, and publishing 0.42 and 0.58 for the 3-node cells would have looked like a precise measurement of wasted capacity while resting on a cap that does not bind. DEVICE-BUSY OCCUPANCY REMAINS THE ONE VALIDATED MEASURE - it counts a device as busy or not, which cannot exceed 1.0 by construction and needs no assumption about how many tasks a device will accept. AND THERE IS A REAL CONSEQUENCE FOR THE GOAL, WHICH IS WHY THIS IS WORTH THE COMMIT RATHER T