F482|RETRACTING F481 - THE WEIGHT IS A HARD CAP AFTER ALL, MY PROBE NEVER CLOSED A SPAN AT A RETRY, AND SLOT UTILISATION IS THE SHARPEST NUMBER YET|F481 concluded that per-device weight is not a hard cap, because a 2-slot cell showed PEAK CONCURRENCY 3 and a slot utilisation of 1.0047. I checked the source afterwards and pick_device filters on d.in_flight less than d.cfg.weight, so the main dispatch path DOES respect the weight - which made my own observation the suspect rather than the engine. THE TELL WAS THAT baseline-n1-r1 SPENT 816 SECONDS OVER TWO CONCURRENT WHILE baseline-n1-r0 SPENT ZERO. The difference is not capacity: n1-r1's retries were on verify-e2e::0 and test-meridian, MID-RUN tasks with siblings alive beside them, while n1-r0's only retry was on integrate-verify, the TERMINAL sink with nothing running next to it. THE CAUSE IS MY SPAN RECONSTRUCTION. I paired task_dispatched with task_completed and never closed a span at task_retry, so a retried task stayed "running" across the retry gap and through every later attempt, overlapping tasks it never actually shared a slot with. CLOSING THE SPAN AT A RETRY FIXES IT COMPLETELY AND THE CONTROL IS EXACT: peak becomes 2 on a 2-slot fleet and 6 on a 6-slot fleet, with ZERO time over the cap in both 1-node cells. THE WEIGHT BINDS. F481'S HEADLINE IS FALSE AND IS RETRACTED, and with it the worry that every earlier six-slot statement rested on a bound that does not hold - F466's width-versus-slots comparison was measured against the RIGHT bound and needs no apology after all. WHAT THE CORRECTION BUYS IS THE MEASURE F480 SAID WAS MISSING, AND IT IS SHARPER THAN DEVICE-BUSY. SLOT UTILISATION against DEVICE OCCUPANCY: n1-r0 0.7729 against 1.0000; n1-r1 0.8944 against 1.0000; n3-r0 0.3551 against 0.5535; n3-r1 0.5297 against 0.8256; n3-r2 0.3878 against 0.6678. STILL NO OVERLAP BETWEEN THE ARMS. And it says something device-busy could not: THE 1-NODE FLEET IS NOT ACTUALLY SATURATED EITHER - its machine is busy 100 percent of the window but only 77 to 89 percent of its SLOTS are filled, so the perfect 1.0000 was hiding real headroom. On three nodes only 35 to 53 percent of slots carry work. ⚠️ FOUR SEPARATE PROBE FAILURES TODAY HAVE NOW BEEN CAUGHT BY A CONTROL OR AN IMPOSSIBLE VALUE, AND IN EVERY CASE THE INSTRUMENT WAS WRONG BEFORE THE ENGINE WAS. ⇒ L288 A SPAN MUST BE CLOSED BY EVERY EVENT THAT ENDS IT, NOT ONLY BY THE HAPPY ONE - A RETRY ENDS AN ATTEMPT, AND A PROBE THAT IGNORES IT INVENTS CONCURRENCY THAT NEVER HAPPENED
F483|I HAVE FLIP-FLOPPED TWICE ON THE SAME QUESTION AND I AM STOPPING - SLOT UTILISATION IS UNRESOLVED AND WILL NOT BE PUBLISHED|F481 said the per-device weight is not a hard cap, on a peak-3 reading from a 2-slot cell. F482 retracted that, because my hand-rolled spans never closed at task_retry and closing them gave peak 2. NOW occupancy.py - the validated instrument, with the same retry-closing fix applied - STILL REPORTS 816 SECONDS AT THREE CONCURRENT TASKS ON A TWO-SLOT FLEET. The two probes disagree and I cannot tell which is right, so I am not going to decide by writing a third version. WHAT IS ESTABLISHED. pick_device filters d.in_flight less than d.cfg.weight, so the MAIN dispatch path respects the cap. But scheduler.rs line 550 records that the dispatch paths DRIFTED APART BEFORE - pick_device learned speed-weight routing while the repair path did not - so a SECOND dispatch path exists and I have not read whether it checks capacity. That is the live hypothesis and it would make F481 right after all, which is exactly why I must not guess. WHAT IS UNAFFECTED, AND IT MATTERS BECAUSE EVERY CLAIM I MADE TODAY RESTS ON IT: DEVICE-BUSY OCCUPANCY IS ROBUST TO THIS ENTIRE DISPUTE. It unions a device's spans and asks only whether that device was busy, so it is bounded by 1.0 BY CONSTRUCTION whether two tasks or three shared the machine. The 1.0000 versus 0.6896 separation, and every occupancy number in F465, F472, F476, F479 and F480, stands regardless of how this resolves. WHAT IS SUSPENDED: slot utilisation. The numbers I computed in F482 - 0.7729, 0.8944, 0.3551, 0.5297 - ARE WITHDRAWN. They were derived from a span reconstruction that disagrees with the file's own, and one of the two is wrong. I will not report a utilisation until the second dispatch path is read and the disagreement is settled. I ALSO RE-MADE A DOCUMENTED MISTAKE TODAY AND SHOULD SAY SO PLAINLY: occupancy.py carries a comment describing this exact failure - pairing dispatch index i with complet