F485|THE GUARD EXISTED FOR HUMANS AND I WAS NOT ONE - AN IMPOSSIBILITY THAT ONLY PRINTS IS NOT A GUARD|occupancy.py has always flagged a concurrency above the summed device weights, printing IMPOSSIBLE, INDICTS THIS SWEEP beside the offending row of its human report. IT WAS DOING THAT CORRECTLY THE WHOLE TIME. What it did not do was return anything a PROGRAM could test: analyse() handed back concurrency_secs with no signal whatsoever, so a caller reading that dict directly received a physically impossible distribution and no indication that the file already knew it was impossible. I WAS THAT CALLER. I read analyse() programmatically, derived a slot utilisation of 1.0047 from a two-slot cell showing three concurrent tasks, and published it in F481 as evidence that the per-device weight is not a hard cap - a claim I then retracted in F482, re-opened in F483 and finally settled in F484 by reading the dispatch path. THE FILE HAD BEEN TELLING ME FOR THE WHOLE OF THAT SEQUENCE, IN A CHANNEL I NEVER LOOKED AT. Shipped: analyse() now returns impossible_concurrency, the sorted list of concurrency levels exceeding slot_count. CONTROLLED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS BEFORE BEING TRUSTED - it fires with 3 on baseline-n1-r1, the known-bad cell, and returns EMPTY on all four others including both 6-slot 3-node cells whose peak is exactly 6 and therefore legal. A flag that fired everywhere would be as useless as one that fired nowhere. THE UNDERLYING ARTEFACT IS STILL UNFIXED AND I AM NOT PRETENDING OTHERWISE: something in the span reconstruction still produces 3-on-2 on that one cell, closing attempts at task_retry did not move it, and slot utilisation STAYS WITHDRAWN until it is found. What is fixed is that the next reader - me in an hour, or anyone else - CANNOT quote the number without the data itself objecting. THAT IS THE SIXTH INSTRUMENT FAILURE TODAY AND THE PATTERN IS NOW UNMISTAKABLE: blind zeros from wrong nesting, a false negative from a byte search, a liveness rule that fired o