F489|THE 3-ON-2 IS REAL IN THE EVENT STREAM AND A JUDGE KILL IS WHY - IT RE-DISPATCHES WITHOUT EMITTING task_retry|I stopped hand-rolling spans (L289) and read occupancy.py's OWN exported _spans, which put the peak at test-api, verify-e2e::0 and verify-e2e::1, all on worksmacstudio, a WEIGHT-2 device. Then I read the raw events for exactly those three instead of reconstructing anything, and the sequence is unambiguous. test-api was dispatched at 47.9 min, judged OVER_READING at 55.5 and RE-DISPATCHED IN THE SAME SECOND, judged over_reading again at 63.1, and re-dispatched a third time at 76.7 before finally being ACCEPTED at 88.2 and completing. AT NO POINT DOES A task_retry EVENT APPEAR FOR ANY OF IT. A JUDGE KILL RE-DISPATCHES A TASK AND EMITS ONLY judge_verdict PLUS task_dispatched, so every probe that closes an attempt on task_retry - including the fix I applied to occupancy.py earlier tonight - IS BLIND TO THE MOST COMMON RE-DISPATCH IN THE ENGINE. That is why closing at retry changed no number. occupancy.py is nevertheless CORRECT here, because it also closes an attempt at the NEXT DISPATCH of the same task, which catches judge kills that task_retry misses. So its spans are right and the three tasks genuinely overlap: at 70 minutes test-api's second attempt (55.5 to 76.7), verify-e2e::0 (60.1 to 83.2) and verify-e2e::1 (63.1 to 76.7) are all dispatched with no intervening completion, on a device whose weight is 2. THE EVENT STREAM SAYS THE DEVICE HELD THREE. WHAT I CANNOT YET SAY IS WHETHER THE DEVICE WAS TRULY OVERSUBSCRIBED. pick_device filters in_flight below weight, so either the judge-kill path releases in_flight while the abandoned worker keeps generating - in which case the machine really is running three streams and the weight is not enforced in practice - or it decrements correctly and the abandoned worker stops, in which case the log OVERSTATES concurrency because an abandoned attempt is counted until its replacement is dispatched. BOTH ARE CONSISTEN