F491|THE JUDGE DIAGNOSED A LITERAL SYNTAX ERROR THIRTEEN TIMES AND THREW THE DIAGNOSIS AWAY EVERY TIME - ONCE THE INTERVENTION CAP IS SPENT NO BRANCH CAN ACT AND THE HINT IS NOT EVEN KEPT|Watching the live 3-node cell past the 21-of-21 mark, test-meridian-edge drew a judge verdict roughly every 70 seconds from 103 to 117 minutes, back to back, each one occupying a node on gabee. The verdicts alternate ok, spec_drift, broken_code, ok, ok, spec_drift, broken_code, spec_drift, ok - and EVERY SINGLE ONE carries action observed. Reading apply_judge_outcome settles why, by exhaustion over its four branches. Accept requires outcome.deterministic. terminal requires outcome.deterministic. redispatch requires interv below max_interventions_per_task and this task has spent both. is_split requires split_enabled which is false by default. A NON-DETERMINISTIC VERDICT ON A CAP-EXHAUSTED TASK CANNOT REACH ANY ACTING BRANCH, SO THE MODEL CALL IS PROVABLY INERT AS AN ACTION. Meanwhile pick_judge_target deliberately WAIVES the rejudge cooldown for a cap-exhausted task, with the stated reason so its terminal-fail stays prompt - and the terminal-fail it is protecting is unreachable on the model path for exactly the reason above. Two clauses in two functions, each correct alone, jointly guaranteeing an uncooled call that can change nothing. BUT THE INERT CALL IS NOT THE REAL LOSS. The hints are excellent and consistent: the file has a syntax error, raise RuntimeError from Non is missing the e in None, and the tests mock urllib3 while the backend uses stdlib urllib.request per spec. That is the most specific, most current thing the entire run knows about this task. prior_hints is written ONLY inside the redispatch branch, so an observed verdict stores NOTHING, and the attempt that eventually replaces this one - started by worker_timeout, a TIMER - will carry the stale hint from the last kill instead. THIS IS MIHAI'S 09:05 DIRECTIVE INVERTED. He asked that a timer trigger a judge DECISION rather than a guillotine. Here the judge decides, correctly and repeatedly, and the engine drops the decision on the floor and lets the timer act uninformed. SHIPPED, ONE CHANGE ONLY, NO CONTROL FLOW TOUCHED: an observed problem verdict on a still-live attempt now stores its hint in prior_hints, so the NEXT dispatch of that task carries it as a supervisor note whatever ends the current attempt. It kills nothing, fails nothing and re-queues nothing - it only makes the replacement attempt an informed one. I DELIBERATELY DID NOT ALSO SILENCE THE CAP-EXHAUSTED JUDGE, even though I proved it inert, because with the hint preserved the call stops being inert and becomes the run's freshest steering signal, and because the cooldown waiver still protects a reachable DETERMINISTIC terminal-fail. PREDICTION REGISTERED BEFORE ANY CELL RUNS ON IT, WITH ITS INERT CASE: on a cell built from this commit, a task that draws a judge_verdict with action observed and a non-empty hint and is later re-dispatched will have that hint delivered, so rules_delivered or the next attempt's supervisor note will differ from the hint of its last re_dispatch. INERT IF no cap-exhausted observed verdict with a non-empty hint occurs, which happened in only one of six cells so far. INERT IS NOT A PASS. ⇒ L297 A SUPERVISOR THAT HAS RUN OUT OF LEVERS HAS NOT RUN OUT OF INFORMATION - WHEN AN OBSERVATION CANNOT BE ACTED ON, KEEP IT FOR WHOEVER ACTS NEXT
F492|I ALMOST SHIPPED A THRESHOLD CHANGE ON A 16-OF-16 CORRELATION AND THE CALIBRATION DATA SAYS MY EXPLANATION WAS BACKWARDS - THEN THE SAMPLE TURNED OUT TO BE BLIND TO THE ONLY POPULATION THAT COULD DECIDE IT|The live cell's tail task test-meridian-edge ran from 42.8 to 123.0 minutes - EIGHTY POINT TWO MINUTES ACROSS FIVE ATTEMPTS - while the other 21 tasks were done by 94. Four attempts were DISCARDED: two deterministic over_reading kills at 50.6 and 58.1, then two 420-second stall retries at 85.4 and 94.5, and only the fifth was accepted. FIFTY-ONE POINT SEVEN MINUTES OF WORK DELETED ON ONE TASK, on a run whose whole wall is 123. That is L278 at its most extreme measured value. I then measured every ACTED deterministic over_reading kill across all seven cells and got 15 test tasks to 1 non-test - and the one non-test is integrate-verify, which OWNS tests/test_integration.py, so SIXTEEN OF SIXTEEN LANDED ON A WORKER WHOSE DELIVERABLE IS A TEST FILE, and ZERO ever hit an implementer. The mechanism looked legible: a test author must read the module under test before it can assert anything, so a gate that trips at 16 tool calls before first write punishes exactly the behaviour test authoring requires. I was about to raise the threshold for test tasks. THE CALIBRATION REFUTES ME. Successful test tasks use a MEDIAN OF 5 TOOL CALLS AND A MAXIMUM OF 13 - every single one under the threshold - against a median of 4 and a max of 31 for non-test, 8 percent of which reach 16. A healthy test author does NOT need many tool calls. So the correct reading of 16-of-16 is the OPPOSITE of mine: test tasks are the population that THRASHES, not the population the gate mis-fires on, which is precisely what the engine's own comment already said when it recorded test-authors as 93 percent of every failure. The correlation was real and my causal story for it was wrong. THEN THE SAMPLE ITSELF FAILED. TaskCompleted emitted from the judge paths hard-codes tool_calls to an EMPTY VECTOR, so every judge-accepted task reports zero tool calls and my distribution silently dropped it. NINE OF TWENTY-TWO SUCCESSFUL TEST TASKS ARE INVISIBLE THAT WAY, AND SIX OF THOSE NINE WERE MULTI-ATTEMPT, against two of seventeen for non-test. THE TASKS MISSING FROM THE CALIBRATION ARE EXACTLY THE STRUGGLING ONES THAT WOULD DECIDE IT. So I am NOT changing the threshold, in either direction, and I am recording that the question is UNANSWERABLE WITH THE CURRENT INSTRUMENT rather than answering it. WHAT I AM SHIPPING IS THE HOLE ITSELF, BECAUSE IT IS WORSE THAN A BLIND SPOT IN MY PROBE. All five TaskCompleted and AttemptRecord emits inside apply_judge_outcome hard-code elapsed_ms to ZERO - accept, kill, salvage and terminal-fail alike - while the elapsed seconds are computed at the top of that very function and sit in scope. The run report then does per_device busy_ms plus equals a.elapsed_ms over the whole attempt history, so EVERY JUDGE-TERMINATED ATTEMPT CONTRIBUTES ZERO NODE-SECONDS TO ITS DEVICE, and TaskOutcome.elapsed_ms reads the LAST attempt, so a task ending in a judge accept reports ZERO MILLISECONDS FOR ITSELF. test-meridian-edge, the 80-minute task, is recorded by the engine as having taken no time at all on three of its five attempts. per_device.busy_ms is the ENGINE'S OWN ANSWER to how busy each node was - the exact question the whole node-scaling goal turns on - and it silently drops the commonest restart in the engine, which F489 established is the judge kill. This is why occupancy.py had to be built from dispatch spans in the first place. PREDICTION REGISTERED BEFORE THE FIX RUNS, WITH ITS INERT CASE: on a cell built from this commit, every task whose final attempt ended in a judge verdict will report a NON-ZERO elapsed_ms, and the sum of per_device busy_ms will EXCEED what the same cell would have reported before. INERT IF the cell records no judge-terminated attempt, which has happened in one of seven cells so far. INERT IS NOT A PASS. ⇒ L298 WHEN A CORRELATION IS SIXTEEN OUT OF SIXTEEN, CHECK WHETHER THE INSTRUMENT CAN SEE THE CASES THAT WOULD REFUTE IT - A PERFECT SIGNAL IS MORE OFTEN A BLIND SAMPLE THAN A REAL LAW
F493|THE STALLS WERE NOT THE AGENT - A WORKER'S ABORTED ATTEMPT ORPHANS THE PROCESS IT SPAWNED, AND ELEVEN OF THEM WERE BURNING FIFTY CPU-MINUTES ON THE MACHINE THAT SERVES THE FLEET|While swarm-3node-r0 was scoring I listed detached processes and found SEVEN ORPHAN LEADERS IN THREE PROCESS GROUPS, ELEVEN PROCESSES TOTAL, WITH FIFTY CPU-MINUTES ALREADY BURNED. Two were pytest runs SPINNING at 39 and 63 percent of a core for 55 and 48 minutes, having consumed 20m49s and 29m00s of CPU. The other five were vendorsync SERVERS FROM EARLIER CELLS still holding ports 18002, 18098, 18099, 18766 and 18768, aged five hours, four and a half, four, three and three. THE START TIMES ARE THE FINDING. Run minute 85.4 and run minute 94.5 are the two task_retry events on test-meridian-edge, both reading agent stalled - no progress for 420s. The two orphan pytest pipelines started 53 and 45 minutes before I looked, which places them AT THOSE TWO MINUTES. THE AGENT DID NOT STALL. The worker ran pytest, pytest never returned, the worker emitted no tokens while waiting on it, the watchdog called that a stall and restarted the attempt - AND NOBODY KILLED THE PYTEST. The replacement attempt ran the same command and made ANOTHER ORPHAN. That is a compounding leak where every stall retry both DELETES the attempt's work and ADDS a permanent CPU parasite. The mechanism is that a judge kill or a stall retry aborts the worker's tokio future, which cancels the await but does not signal the OS process the shell tool spawned; the child reparents to init and runs forever. AND THE JUDGE HAD ALREADY DIAGNOSED THE ROOT CAUSE. At minute 103.2 it said the tests mock urllib3 while the backend uses stdlib urllib.request per spec - so a test intending to fake the HTTP layer fails to intercept it and blocks on something real. One of the two orphans is holding FIVE LISTENING TCP SOCKETS on localhost, which is a test fixture that started servers and never tore them down. F491 is why that diagnosis went nowhere