F523|THE BUILD-PURE PAIR IS NOW n EQUALS 4 VERSUS 4 AND READS BOTH PILLARS - AND THE HONEST HEADLINE IS STILL THE BAND, NOT THE GAP|A fourth 1-node cell landed on `071b57514` and the build-pure comparison is now FOUR VERSUS FOUR. 1-node: 0.9443/68, 0.9650/82, 0.3587/108, 0.6876/99. 3-node: 0.7426/78, 0.9833/72, 0.4624/100, 0.7703/79. **QUALITY PLUS 0.0008. SPEED 0.92x. VERDICT: BOTH PILLARS.** That is the third revision of this headline in twelve hours and every revision came from more data, never from a new argument: at n equals 1 I reported one node beating three on both pillars; at n equals 4 versus 3 quality was minus 0.0163 and the verdict SPEED ONLY; at four versus four quality is PLUS 0.0008 and the verdict is BOTH PILLARS. AND I AM NOT GOING TO LEAD WITH THAT (L321). A quality gap of 0.0008 sits inside a MEASURED multi-node spread of 0.5209 — it is six hundred times smaller than the noise it lives in — and the speed gap of 0.92x sits inside the same arm's own 1.96x fastest-to-slowest ratio. goal.py prints both caveats and it is right to. THE DEFENSIBLE STATEMENT IS THAT QUALITY IS DEAD EVEN AND THREE NODES IS ABOUT EIGHT PERCENT FASTER, WITH NEITHER GAP CLEARING ITS BAND. Not a win; the absence of the loss I spent the night trying to engineer away. AND THE SWEEP'S OWN TABLE MOVED THE SAME WAY, WHICH IS THE PART THAT MAKES IT WORTH REPORTING. It now reads 1-node n equals 8 at 73.9 percent and 89 minutes against 3-node n equals 8 at 74.0 percent and 82 minutes — so on the pooled-across-builds view three nodes is now marginally AHEAD on quality and seven minutes faster, having been 1.6 points behind when I read it two hours ago. TWO INSTRUMENTS BUILT ON OPPOSITE PRINCIPLES — ONE POOLED, ONE BUILD-PURE — HAVE NOW CONVERGED FROM DIFFERENT DIRECTIONS ONTO THE SAME ANSWER. That agreement is the strongest thing this campaign has, and it is only visible because I finally printed them together. SHIPPED SO I CANNOT MISS IT AGAIN: goal.py now prints the sweep's own arm table beside its build-pure rows, with the disagreement stated inline — the sweep pools across builds (the F479 error that once forced a retraction) while these rows are build-pure and therefore smaller-n, so NEITHER IS STRICTLY BETTER and the value is in whether they agree. ⚠️ NONE OF THIS TESTS TONIGHT'S FIXES. Every one of these eight cells ran `071b57514`, which PREDATES all four engine commits now in the binary. The first cell on the new build started at 08:59 with an ETA near 10:52 and no `spec_repair_wave` has fired yet. ⇒ L329 WHEN A HEADLINE HAS BEEN REVISED THREE TIMES BY DATA ALONE, THE NUMBER WAS NEVER THE FINDING - THE BAND WAS, AND IT WAS THE SAME BAND EVERY TIME
F524|I BAKED A LEVER ON AND LEFT IT OUT OF THE EVENT THAT SAYS WHICH LEVERS RAN - THE EXACT MISTAKE THAT FILE'S OWN COMMENT WAS WRITTEN TO PREVENT|The first cell on the new binary confirms the rebuild took: its levers_resolved carries build_sha d91fd8b96, my spec_repair commit. AND IN THE SAME EVENT, `spec_repair` IS ABSENT. I flipped a lever to default-ON four hours ago and never added it to the hand-maintained list that tells a run which levers it ran. THE COMMENT DIRECTLY BELOW THE LINE I EDITED SAYS THIS IN CAPITALS: two levers shipped without a line here, and the log could not say whether either was on; a new gate is invisible until someone adds it, and ABSENT FROM THE EVENT READS EXACTLY LIKE RESOLVED TO NULL. It even names the measured case — act_now_nudge came back None from an engine where it defaults ON, indistinguishable from the lever silently not shipping. I read that comment this morning while grepping for the emit and shipped the same defect anyway. WHY IT WOULD HAVE COST ME THE ANSWER. The whole point of baking spec_repair was to learn whether racing repair attempts helps. If the cell now running finishes with NO spec_repair_wave, there are two explanations — the lever is dead, or the cell never entered repair — and without a line in levers_resolved I CANNOT TELL THEM APART FROM THE LOG. Two of seven archived cells never entered repair, so that is not a hypothetical branch, it is a 29 percent chance of an unresolvable result. F517's check already distinguishes those cases using pool size and complete_fix_dispatched, but it infers; the levers event STATES. An instrument that infers a lever's state when the engine could simply report it is the weaker of two available answers. FIXED, ONE LINE, `"spec_repair": spec_repair()`. 467 tests pass. ⚠️ COMMITTED, NOT BUILT — the release binary is untouched at 1786167678 and the freeze holds. Rebuilding now would reset the backlog and abandon the very measurement this line exists to protect, which is F507's lesson applied to a change that improves observability rather than behaviour: the fix rides the NEXT rebuild, and until then I can still prove the lever is on by grepping the binary (L324), which I already did. ⇒ L330 A LEVER YOU BAKE IS A LEVER YOU MUST ADD TO THE EVENT THAT LISTS LEVERS - OTHERWISE THE RUN CANNOT SAY WHETHER IT HAD IT, AND ABSENT LOOKS IDENTICAL TO OFF
F525|I AM ABOUT TO MEASURE A LEVER WHOSE COST IS INVISIBLE - THE SUFFIX HAS NO NODE-SECOND INSTRUMENT, AND I WILL NOT BUILD ONE AGAINST EVENT SHAPES I HAVE NEVER SEEN|Two checks while the first new-binary cell plans. FIRST, THE SAFETY CLAIM HELD. I have called spec_repair MONOTONIC BY CONSTRUCTION all morning on the strength of reading ten lines of pick_repair_winner. It is genuinely tested — eight assertions covering the strict win, the all-worse case, the TIE at baseline, all-None, None-mixed-with-Some, the earliest-wins tie-break, the empty slice, and a baseline of zero that nothing can beat. So the argument stands, and I was right by reading rather than by checking, which is luck. I should have grepped for the test BEFORE asserting the property four times in commit messages. SECOND, AND THIS IS THE REAL GAP: THE SUFFIX HAS NO FLEET-COST INSTRUMENT. busy_node_secs and execute_occupancy are both computed from dispatch spans inside the EXECUTE window. post_execute_secs, which I shipped this morning, measures the suffix's DURATION and nothing else. So when spec_repair races N attempts, the suffix may get SHORTER while consuming N times the node-seconds, and every instrument I own would report that as an unqualified win. That is exactly the shape of trade Mihai's two pillars exist to expose, and I would have missed it. I AM DELIBERATELY NOT BUILDING THE INSTRUMENT YET, AND THE REASON IS THE CAMPAIGN'S OLDEST LESSON. I do not know what the raced path emits. The spec_repair branch REPLACES the serial dispatch, so it may not emit complete_fix_dispatched at all, and the only wave event I have read is a single point carrying twins and verified with no interval. Building a node-second estimator against event shapes I have never observed is how this campaign produced five blind zeros — a probe that looks right, reads a key that never exists, and returns a confident number. THE FIRST RACED CELL WILL SHOW ME THE SHAPE, AND L318 REQUIRES ME TO INSTRUMENT IT THAT HOUR. Registering the intent now so it cannot be forgotten, and so the omission is on the record rather than discovered later as a silent gap. PREDICTION, WITH ITS INERT CASE AND A RATE RATHER THAN AN EXISTENCE (L327): on the first cell where a spec_repair_wave fires, the wave's `twins` field will be at least 2 and at most the fleet's model count, and the suffix will contain MORE than one fix worker's node-seconds — provable from the activity digests, which write one file per agent, so a raced round leaves MORE THAN ONE digest for the same round. FALSIFIED if a wave fires with twins under 2, or if the digests show only one repair agent. INERT if no cell enters repair, which happened in 2 of 7 archived cells. INERT IS NOT A PASS. ⇒ L331 BEFORE MEASURING A LEVER, ASK WHAT IT COSTS AND WHETHER ANYTHING MEASURES THAT - A CHANGE THAT SHORTENS A WINDOW WHILE TRIPLING ITS COMPUTE READS AS A PURE WIN TO EVERY INSTRUMENT THAT ONLY TIMES THE WINDOW
F526|THE ORPHAN CHAIN FIRED IN ANGER FOR THE FIRST TIME AND CAUGHT A CROSS-CELL PORT SQUATTER 74 MINUTES INTO THE NEXT RUN|Routine hygiene during the new-binary cell surfaced exactly what the tooling was built for, unattended and without me looking for it. reap.py's HUNG MID-RUN section — the one I added because F502 proved a hung child is invisible to an orphan scan while its parent lives — flagged pid 83936, BLOCKED, 74.5 minutes old, HOLDING PORT 18944. Its parent was `bash -c cd swarm-1node-r3 && python3 -m vendorsync`, which is the PREVIOUS cell's app server, still running seventy-four minutes after that cell ended and twenty-seven minutes into the next one. THAT IS PRECISELY THE CROSS-CELL CONTAMINATION F493 MEASURED AND F506 DREW THE LINE AROUND: within-cell mess the engine made is DATA, cross-cell survival is an ARTEFACT and removing it is obligatory. THEN THE GUARD MADE THE DECISION INSTEAD OF ME, WHICH IS THE WHOLE POINT OF F509. `--kill` found the live engine at 27.9 minutes, confirmed EVERY target predates it, printed "cross-cell, safe to clear", and removed seven process groups. Had any target been younger than the live engine it would have REFUSED with exit 3 and named the pids. I did not have to decide, and F509 exists because the one time I did decide by hand I fired the rule two minutes late. A rule that lives in a tool cannot be executed late. ⚠️ THE SCAN FOUND SEVEN, NOT ONE. The HUNG MID-RUN section reports only children whose parent is still alive; the ORPHAN scan found six more that had already reparented to init. Two different populations, two different detectors, and reading only the section that caught my eye would have left six behind. ⚠️ AND IT VALIDATES NOTHING ABOUT QUALITY. This is hygiene, not an engine improvement — it removes a confound from the measurement rather than making the swarm better. The port squatter would have collided with any test in the running cell that wanted 18944, and that collision would have been recorded as a swarm defect. What I have prevented is a FALSE FINDING, which is worth exactly as much as a real fix and no more. ⇒ L332 THE VALUE OF AN INSTRUMENT IS NOT THE FINDING IT PUBLISHES, IT IS THE FALSE FINDING IT PREVENTS - THIS ONE STOPPED A PREVIOUS CELL'S SERVER FROM BEING RECORDED AS THE NEXT CELL'S BUG
F527|MIHAI ASKED WHY THE SLOWEST NODE HAD THE LAST TASK - THE ANSWER WAS NOT THE BAN I ALMOST FIXED, AND CHECKING THE POOL EVENT SAVED ME FROM SHIPPING A NO-OP ON A WRONG PREMISE|The trace is unambiguous. test-api-error-handling went to the fast worksmacstudio node at 44.0 min, was killed over_reading deterministic at 51.2, went to the fast local-mihai node, was killed over_reading again at 60.3, and its third dispatch landed on mac-gabee — the SLOWEST host — where it ran 29 minutes drawing nothing but ok verdicts while both faster nodes reported READY. It was the last task in the run, so the whole run waited on the slowest node. MY FIRST DIAGNOSIS WAS THE avoid_device BAN AND IT WAS WRONG. I wrote the fix, wrote its test, and it passed: skip the ban when the fleet has one model, since a judge verdict indicts the WORKER not the host and moving cannot change the sample. THEN I CHECKED THE EVENT THAT NAMES THE STATE (L326) BEFORE COMMITTING AND THE POOL REPORTS THREE DISTINCT model_id VALUES — gabee, mihai-qwen3.6-…, qwen3.6-… — because DISTINCT IDENTIFIERS ARE HOW LM LINK ROUTES TO DIFFERENT HOSTS. So distinct_models is 3, the ban would still have applied, AND MY FIX WOULD HAVE CHANGED NOTHING WHILE READING AS A FIX. That is the eighth blind-premise near-miss of this campaign and the first where I had already written the code and the test. RE-READING THE DISPATCHES KILLED THE DIAGNOSIS TOO. avoid_device holds ONE device and is OVERWRITTEN each kill, so the third attempt had BOTH gabee and worksmacstudio available — worksmacstudio was no longer banned. It went to gabee because test-meridian-resilience was dispatched IN THE SAME INSTANT and took the fast node, leaving one slot. NOTHING WAS MALFUNCTIONING. The ranking simply had no reason to prefer a twice-killed task over a fresh one competing for the same slot. THE REAL FIX USES THE ENGINE'S OWN IDIOM. pick_device ALREADY routes HARD tasks to the fastest free node, on exactly the right reasoning stated in its own comment: identical models differ only in host speed, so the heaviest work on the quickest host shortens the critical path. A task on its THIRD attempt has earned the same treatment and was not getting it — it has already consumed two attempts of wall-clock, everything downstream is blocked behind it, and it is empirically the run's tail risk, none of which is true of the fresh task beside it. Shipped dispatch_prefers_fastest_node(is_hard, attempts) = is_hard OR attempts >= 2, wired into the existing `hard` key so load stays primary and nothing over-concentrates. The bar is 2 rather than 1 because single retries are common and cheap and this targets only the case measured to hurt. Six assertions: a fresh easy task must NOT claim the preference (or it means nothing), one retry must not either, attempts 2 and 5 must, and HARD keeps it on every dispatch. 55 + 32 tests pass, clippy clean. ⚠️ WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO: it does not stop a twice-killed task being slow, and it cannot help when the fast nodes are genuinely busy — it only ensures that when a fast node IS free, the tail-risk task gets it ahead of a fresh one. On a saturated fleet it is a no-op by construction. ⇒ L333 WHEN A SCHEDULER PUTS WORK SOMEWHERE SURPRISING, RECONSTRUCT THE CANDIDATE SET AT THAT INSTANT BEFORE BLAMING A RULE - THE BAN I INDICTED HAD ALREADY EXPIRED, AND THE REAL CAUSE WAS TWO DISPATCHES IN THE SAME SECOND
F528|spec_repair FIRED FOR THE FIRST TIME IN THE HISTORY OF THIS BENCH - THREE TWINS, ONE PER MODEL, ON A FOUR-FINDING ROUND|At run-minute 95.8 the COMPLETE loop opened with complete_verify round 0: ran true, passed FALSE, FOUR findings, the leading one the familiar pytest -q failed, the generated tests exercise runtime paths that help and collect-only never invoke. AND IN THE SAME INSTANT THREE complete_fix_dispatched EVENTS: twin 0 to gabee, twin 1 to mihai-qwen3.6, twin 2 to worksmacstudio, each carrying task_id complete-fix::twinN and baseline_findings 4. Three rules_delivered followed, one per twin. THAT IS THE RACE. Before today spec_repair_wave appeared in ZERO of thirteen archived cells; the lever was written, documented, measured across 31 repair rounds by whoever built it, and never once switched on. It is on now and it is using ALL THREE NODES on a single finding set — which is precisely the thing this campaign has spent its entire life failing to find a use for. THE ENGINE'S OWN CLAIM WAS THAT THE AXIS THAT DECOMPOSES IS THE ATTEMPT, NOT THE FINDING, AND THIS IS THAT CLAIM EXECUTING. F517's prediction shape is confirmed on the dispatch side: twins equals 3, which is at least 2 and at most the fleet's model count, and there are three distinct repair agents for one round rather than one. The wave event itself has not fired yet — it is emitted AFTER the race resolves — so the promotion half of the prediction (winner_findings STRICTLY below baseline_findings, or nothing promoted) is still open and is the half that matters for safety. AND IT ANSWERS THE QUESTION I REFUSED TO GUESS AT. F525 declined to build a node-seconds estimator because I did not know what the raced path emits and building against an imagined shape is how this campaign produced five blind zeros. The shape is now observed: complete_fix_dispatched fires ONCE PER TWIN carrying round, twin, model, task_id and baseline_findings. So the suffix's fleet cost is computable after all — sum of per-twin dispatch-to-completion intervals — and L318 requires me to build it this hour rather than after the cell lands. ⚠️ WHAT IS NOT YET KNOWN AND MUST NOT BE ASSUMED: whether any twin beats the baseline, whether promotion is strictly better as pick_repair_winner's tests require, and whether racing three attempts costs three times the node-seconds or less. A lever firing is not a lever working — FIRED IS NOT CORRECT, and this campaign has a rule about exactly that. ⚠️ Also observed: all three twins got rules_delivered with kind test-author and tailored TRUE, so the repair workers are receiving the tailored test-author rules including the blocking-server rule shipped this morning. That is a second change landing in the same window and I must not attribute the round's outcome to either one alone. ⇒ L334 A LEVER THAT HAS NEVER RUN TELLS YOU NOTHING UNTIL THE FIRST TIME IT DOES - AND THE FIRST FIRING IS WHERE YOU LEARN THE EVENT SHAPE YOU REFUSED TO GUESS
F529|THE REPAIR SUFFIX IS THE MOST SATURATED PHASE OF THE ENTIRE RUN AND THE RACE COSTS 2.67 NODES - BOTH FACTS WERE UNMEASURABLE UNTIL I STOPPED USING THE EVENT LOG|F525 refused to build the node-seconds estimator because the raced path's event shape was unknown, and F528 then observed it. But building on that shape would have been wrong anyway: complete_fix_dispatched has NO MATCHING COMPLETION EVENT. Its keys are round, twin, model, task_id, baseline_findings and nothing else, so there is no dispatch-to-completion interval to sum. The event channel is blind to the suffix BY CONSTRUCTION and no amount of care with it would have helped — occupancy.py derives busy time from task_dispatched to task_completed pairs that the repair loop never emits. THE ANSWER WAS AN INDEPENDENT CHANNEL THAT WAS ALREADY RUNNING. fleetsample.sh has been polling lms ps every 30 seconds this whole campaign and recording what each node is actually doing, engine events or not. 13153 samples, covering every phase of every cell. suffixcost.py reads that channel and the suffix becomes measurable without the engine's cooperation. MEASURED ON THE LIVE RACE: prefix occupancy 0.661, execute 0.798, SUFFIX 0.881. The repair suffix is the MOST heavily used phase of the run, not the idle tail I had been picturing, and that inverts the assumption behind every fanout idea I have had about filling the tail — there is no tail to fill. The race itself burned 31.6 node-minutes over 11.9 minutes of wall, an average of 2.67 nodes busy, split almost evenly at 10.4 for gabee, 9.9 for mihai and 11.4 for worksmacstudio. So racing three twins really does cost close to three times a serial fix, and that is now a number rather than a worry. TWO INSTRUMENT DEFECTS CAUGHT BEFORE PUBLISHING, BOTH OF WHICH WOULD HAVE PRODUCED A CONFIDENT FALSE FINDING. First, BUSY IS NOT GENERATING. My own shell check counted GENERATING and reported ONE node busy during a three-way race; gabee was PROCESSINGPROMPT, which is prompt ingestion on a real host holding a real slot. Reporting that the race collapsed to one node would have been the most dramatic finding of the campaign and it would have been false. Second, THE DENOMINATOR. The first working version divided busy node-count by the pool's SLOT count of 6, mixing a node-count numerator with a slot denominator, and a fully saturated fleet reported occupancy 0.437. lms ps prints one status per identifier, so the numerator can never exceed 3 and the ceiling was structurally 0.5. Its own output exposed it: peak 3 of 6. AND THE CONTROL I FIRST WROTE PASSED FOR THE WRONG REASON. I checked the sampler against the event channel with an arbitrary within-0.15 tolerance; it passed at 0.530 versus 0.399 while the sampler figure was itself deflated by the bad denominator. Two errors cancelling into a green. The real relationship is not equality at all — events measure SLOT occupancy and the sampler measures NODE utilisation, and since any busy slot makes its node busy, node utilisation is greater than or equal to slot occupancy ALWAYS. That inequality is a fact about the two definitions rather than a tolerance I chose, it cannot be tuned to pass, and it now reads 0.530 at most 0.798, consistent. Controls both directions plus vacuous-truth, header-leak, sampler-outage, midnight-rollover and determinism: PASS. An unsampled window returns None and prints UNMEASURED, never a confident zero. ⚠️ THE SAMPLER IS FLEET-GLOBAL, so these figures are what the fleet was doing and not what this run caused; with one run live they coincide, and the file says so rather than implying attribution it cannot support. ⚠️ AND THE COST NUMBER DECIDES NOTHING ON ITS OWN — 2.67x is only bad if the race fails to shorten the suffix or fails to promote a strictly better result, and the wave has not fired yet. ⇒ L335 WHEN THE EVENT LOG IS STRUCTURALLY BLIND TO A PHASE, STOP TRYING TO READ IT AND FIND THE CHANNEL THAT WAS ALREADY WATCHING - AND CHECK WHETHER YOUR CONTROL PASSES BECAUSE THE INSTRUMENT IS RIGHT OR BECAUSE TWO ERRORS CANCELLED
F530|MY OWN F529 HEADLINE WAS BACKWARDS AND THE CORRECTION IS THE FIRST REAL NODE-SCALING WIN OF THIS CAMPAIGN - THE SERIAL REPAIR SUFFIX RUNS ONE NODE OF THREE FOR TWENTY-ONE MINUTES AND spec_repair FILLS IT|F529 published THE REPAIR SUFFIX IS THE BUSIEST PHASE OF THE RUN off a single live cell at occupancy 0.881. That cell was running a three-way race AT THE MOMENT I MEASURED IT, so the measurement contained its own explanation and I generalised a property of the treatment into a property of the phase. Testing it against the archived cells refuted it immediately. THE SERIAL SUFFIX IS THE IDLEST PHASE OF A THREE-NODE RUN, NOT THE BUSIEST. baseline-n3-r2, 21.6 minutes of suffix on 43 samples: occupancy 0.314 against an execute phase of 0.936. That is ONE NODE OF THREE, and it means two nodes sat doing nothing for twenty-one minutes at the end of a run whose execute phase had just used all three. baseline-n3-r3 agrees in direction at 0.435 against 0.849 on 7 samples. AND THE ONE-NODE CELLS PROVE IT IS A THREE-NODE PATHOLOGY. Their suffix occupancy is 0.316 to 0.333 against an execute occupancy of 0.333, which for a one-node run IS saturation — one of the fleet's three identifiers, busy throughout. A single-node run has no idle tail because it cannot have one. The idle tail appears ONLY when you add nodes, which is precisely the shape of defect this campaign exists to find and the first time I have caught it in the act. THE RACED SUFFIX IS 0.911 ON 32 SAMPLES OVER 16 MINUTES. So spec_repair takes the window where three nodes degenerate to one and puts all three back to work, 0.314 to 0.911. THIS ALSO REVERSES THE COST STORY I TOLD YESTERDAY. F529 framed 2.67 nodes as the price of racing and asked whether the wall-clock saving repaid it. But those nodes were IDLE in the counterfactual — the serial suffix does not use them and cannot use them — so racing three twins in that window costs almost nothing in opportunity terms. L331 asked what the lever costs and the honest answer is: far less than the node-seconds figure suggests, because the resource it consumes has no other bidder. THREE INSTRUMENT DEFECTS FOUND WHILE DOING THIS, ALL IN MY OWN NEW FILE. First, I labelled cells RACED by the mere presence of complete_fix_dispatched, but that event ALSO fires for a serial fix — one dispatch per round — so my first table called four one-node cells raced. The correct test is max twins per round of at least 2, and on that test TODAY IS THE ONLY RACED CELL IN THE BENCH'S HISTORY, which is what F528 claimed and now has a defensible basis. Second, COVERAGE IS NOT RESOLUTION: two archived cells had 48-second suffixes reporting coverage 1.00 and reliable TRUE while resting on 2 and 3 readings, and their occupancies of 0.454 and 0.908 are pure quantisation — one node flipping state moves the figure by a third. I was about to read that spread as a difference between runs. reliable now requires MIN_SAMPLES of 6 as well as coverage, with a control asserting a 90-second fully-covered window is UNRELIABLE while the long window beside it stays reliable. Third, occupancy is measured against the FLEET's three identifiers and not against the nodes a run was allotted, so a one-node run can never exceed 0.333 and cross-arm occupancy comparison is meaningless without that normalisation. ⚠️ THE SAMPLE IS ONE CELL PER ARM. One well-sampled serial three-node suffix, one raced. The direction is large and mechanical rather than marginal, but it is n=1 on each side and the 0.5209 spread rule applies to everything this campaign measures. ⚠️ AND THE PROMOTION HALF IS STILL OPEN — filling an idle window with work is only a win if the winner is STRICTLY better than baseline_findings of 4; free fleet time spent on a tie is still a tie. ⇒ L336 WHEN A MEASUREMENT CONTAINS THE TREATMENT YOU ARE MEASURING, YOU HAVE MEASURED THE TREATMENT AND NOT THE PHASE - GO FIND THE UNTREATED CELLS BEFORE YOU GENERALISE, AND CHECK WHETHER THE RESOURCE A LEVER SPENDS HAD ANY OTHER BIDDER
