F560|THE SILENT WINDOW IS A SECOND DRAFT ROUND THAT EMITS NOTHING - INSTRUMENTED RATHER THAN ASSERTED, BECAUSE I HAVE NAMED A MECHANISM FROM THE SHAPE OF THE SOURCE TWICE TODAY AND BEEN WRONG BOTH TIMES|F559 located an unaccounted window: between plan_convergence and the first detail_completed the engine emits NOTHING for 6.6 to 8.2 minutes, measured at 6.8, 6.7 and 6.6 on three consecutive rounds of the live cell, while the first detail call in that window self-reports eighty seconds. Roughly five and a half minutes per round, about sixteen minutes of a 58.5-minute prefix, invisible. Reading the code between the two line numbers found `draft_round(system2, draft_temp).await` appearing TWICE more after convergence — once on the incremental-replan path and once on the backbone-lock path — and `skeleton_drafts` is emitted for the FIRST round only. THE OCCUPANT IS A SECOND FULL BEST-OF-N DRAFT ROUND OVER THE WHOLE FLEET WITH NO EVENT OF ITS OWN. The arithmetic fits: the live cell's first round reports secs 236, which is 3.9 minutes, and 6.8 minus the 80-second detail leaves 5.5 for a second round sized slightly larger because retarget increments best_of_n. AND I DID NOT STOP THERE AND CALL IT PROVEN. I went looking for stderr, which prints incremental splice not adopted or backbone round-2 adopted and would say WHICH path ran — and there is no stderr capture for these cells. So the code and the timing agree and I still cannot confirm the path. That is exactly the situation in which I have been wrong twice today: F548 thresholded plan_confidence when the gate reads binding_signal agreement, and F553 called the review layer blind when it is merely narrow. Both were named from the shape of the source. SO I SHIPPED THE MEASUREMENT INSTEAD OF THE CLAIM. Both call sites now emit skeleton_drafts_round2 carrying path (incremental or backbone), returned, dead and secs, so the next cell that retargets will say which path ran and what it cost, in its own log, without me inferring