F561|I PREDICTED THE ENGINE'S GATE WAS BLIND TO ITS LARGEST DEFICIT AND THE MEASUREMENT REFUTED ME - IT DISCRIMINATES, BUT IT IS PERMISSIVE|F556 established that tier B, behavioural correctness against the spec, is the largest single deficit in the score at 0.43 to 0.49 against tier A shape at 0.82 to 0.92. Reading `run_smoke_gate` for Python found it produces exactly four kinds of finding: pytest collect-only errors, `pytest -q` FAILING ON THE GENERATED TESTS, `--help` failing, and advertised commands that print an error while exiting zero. NOT ONE of those inspects pagination limits, resync idempotency, chronological ordering, summary accuracy or input validation — which IS tier B. So the hypothesis wrote itself: the engine grades the app against the app's OWN swarm-written tests while the score grades it against the SPEC, and a swarm that writes weak tests would pass its own gate while failing behaviour. STRUCTURALLY BLIND TO ITS LARGEST DEFICIT. THE MEASUREMENT SAYS NO. Joining the engine's own `complete_result.passed` to the scorer's tier B across the five cells that retain a tiers block: the two cells the engine PASSED score tier B 0.917 and 0.500, mean 0.709. The three it FAILED score 0.222, 0.333 and 0.361, mean 0.305. A GAP OF 0.40, AND THE ORDERING IS PERFECT — every passed cell sits above every failed one. The gate's verdict is genuinely informative about behavioural correctness even though none of its checks names a behavioural property. That makes sense in hindsight: an app whose own generated tests fail, or whose imports break, or whose commands exit zero while printing errors, is very unlikely to paginate correctly either. The gate is a PROXY, and on this corpus it is a working one. MY CLAIM IS REFUTED AND I AM GLAD I MEASURED IT. That is the fourth wrong claim caught before publishing today and the third caught by the specific discipline of not naming a mechanism from the shape of the source — F548 thresholded the wrong variable, F553 called a narrow detector blind, F559 was about to rebuild an optimisation that already existed. All four would have been confident, plausible and wrong. WHAT SURVIVES IS WEAKER AND STILL WORTH ACTING ON: THE GATE IS NECESSARY BUT NOT SUFFICIENT. baseline-n3-r3 PASSED the engine's gate with tier B at 0.500 — half the behavioural checks failing on an app the engine called green. So passing is compatible with getting half the spec's behaviour wrong, and the headroom between the gate and the score is exactly the space where a behavioural check would earn its keep. That is a smaller and better-specified claim than blindness. ⚠️ n IS FIVE, two passed against three failed, and a 0.40 gap on two-versus-three points is a direction rather than an effect size. The tier archive shipped in F557 will widen this, but only for cells that land from now on. ⚠️ AND `verified` IS FALSE ON ALL FIVE CELLS INCLUDING BOTH PASSES — the engine never claimed verification even when it claimed green, which is the honest passed-true-verified-false state F531 described, and it means the engine already knows its gate is a proxy. ⇒ L367 A PROXY THAT NAMES NONE OF THE PROPERTIES YOU CARE ABOUT CAN STILL RANK THEM CORRECTLY - TEST WHETHER IT DISCRIMINATES BEFORE CALLING IT BLIND, BECAUSE BLIND AND PERMISSIVE DEMAND COMPLETELY DIFFERENT FIXES
F562|F532 FIRED IN THE WILD ON ITS FIRST OPPORTUNITY - THE STALE 1200 WAS LIFTED TO 3000 AND THIS CELL GETS THE SECOND REPAIR ROUND THE LAST ONE WAS DENIED|F533 predicted, before any cell ran on the frozen binary, that complete_cap_lifted would fire on EVERY cell on this machine because the live config carries the pre-raise complete_cap_secs of 1200. FIRST TEST, CONFIRMED: at run-minute 118.8 the frozen cell emitted complete_cap_lifted with requested_secs 1200, effective_secs 3000, rounds 2, fix_cap_secs 1200. The lift is exactly the arithmetic F532 shipped — a budget that cannot fit two fix rounds of up to 1200 seconds each is raised to the engine's own computed 3000 rather than to a number invented at the call site. AND THE CONSEQUENCE IS THE ONE THAT MATTERS. baseline-n3-r0 scored 0.9033, the best result this campaign has produced, and SHIPPED RED WITH ONE FINDING because its round-0 race ran 1207 seconds against a 1200-second cap and the loop broke at cap_deadline before dispatching the second fix it was budgeted for. This cell opened COMPLETE with the same shape — round 0, ran true, passed FALSE, FOUR findings, three complete_fix_dispatched twins racing gabee, mihai and worksmacstudio — and it now has 3000 seconds instead of 1200, so the round that was denied last time is affordable. That is a measured engine improvement on the exact failure that cost the campaign its best cell. AND THE CHECK CORRECTLY REFUSED TO GRADE IT YET. verdicts.py returned INERT for F532 with the reason this cell never reached the COMPLETE loop, because complete_result has not been emitted — the phase is mid-repair. That is the right answer: the predicate needs the phase's end state to compute unspent budget, and guessing would manufacture either a pass or a fail. An INERT that names its missing precondition is the whole reason the third outcome exists. ⚠️ n IS ONE. The prediction said EVERY cell and one cell has tested it; the falsifier stays open until at least four land. ⚠️ AND THE LIFT IS NOT YET A WIN — it grants budget, it does not spend it well. F531 already showed a repair round can promote nothing, and the twins here start from four findings rather than the one that would have been cheap to clear. The claim is that the round is now REACHABLE, not that it will succeed. ⚠️ A SECOND spec_repair RACE IS ALSO NOW IN FLIGHT, so this cell will carry both F532's extra budget and F517's three-way race, and no outcome can be attributed to either alone. ⇒ L368 A PREDICTION THAT NAMES A MECHANISM EVENT IS SETTLED THE MOMENT THE EVENT FIRES - THE OUTCOME PREDICTION IT SERVES IS NOT, AND CONFLATING THE TWO IS HOW A FIRING LEVER GETS REPORTED AS A WORKING ONE
F563|THE BEHAVIOURAL CHECK I WAS ABOUT TO DESIGN ALREADY EXISTS, IS BAKED ON, FIRES 15 OF 15, AND VERIFIES EXACTLY TWO THINGS - IT IS AIMED RIGHT AND FAR TOO THIN|F561 established the surviving claim that the smoke gate is NECESSARY BUT NOT SUFFICIENT, and I flagged that the legitimate remedy is a check derived from the app's OWN SPEC rather than from the scorer's tier-B list, since mirroring that list would be fitting the engine to the test and would void every score this campaign has produced. Before designing anything I applied L355 and grepped. `spec_contract` ALREADY EXISTS AND ITS OWN DOC COMMENT STATES F561'S CONCLUSION ALMOST VERBATIM: the smoke gate proves the tree IMPORTS and that `python3 -m PKG --help` exits 0, NEVER that the app HONORS its advertised REST contract. It parses the SPEC for advertised endpoints and a port — spec-derived, therefore the legitimate form — and it is baked ON with `spec_contract: Some(true)`. IT FIRES IN FIFTEEN OF FIFTEEN CELLS. And in fourteen of those fifteen it reports `verified 2, findings 0, inconclusive 1`. I ALSO SPOTTED A VACUOUS PASS AND IT HAD ALREADY BEEN FIXED. One archived cell shows `verified 0, findings 0` with the detail every advertised check that bound was satisfied, which the function's own logic forbids — `verified == 0` must report CHECKED NOTHING. Reading the emit site rather than asserting the bug found the fix already in place, with a comment that names the defect better than I would have: the CHECKED NOTHING arm used to require all three counters to be zero INCLUDING `inconclusive`, so recording WHY a check could not conclude pushed the honest path into the success arm — THE MORE CANDIDLY THIS CHECK EXPLAINED ITSELF, THE MORE CERTAINLY IT REPORTED SUCCESS. That cell is pre-fix. FIFTH TIME TODAY I NEARLY PUBLISHED SOMETHING THE ENGINE ALREADY KNEW, and the fifth time reading the source before claiming caught it. WHAT IS GENUINELY OPEN IS THINNESS, NOT ABSENCE. Two verified checks per cell against TWELVE